1
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,280
OK.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:09,800
Welcome back.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:11,980
We're going to move away from pre-training now.

4
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:15,100
And I think we'll get into, in my opinion,

5
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:18,820
some of the messier parts of language modeling.

6
00:00:18,820 --> 00:00:20,460
I mean, data is always pretty messy,

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:22,840
but I think post-training and everything

8
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:28,360
that goes along with it is really artisanal in many ways.

9
00:00:28,360 --> 00:00:31,720
So to set the stage, thus far, I think

10
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:35,360
you can get to a souped up version of GPT-3.

11
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,160
I'm sure you can use more flops, you can get more data.

12
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,500
So you can make something a little bit better than GPT-3.

13
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,120
But ultimately, the amount of utility

14
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:45,620
you can get from something like GPT-3,

15
00:00:45,620 --> 00:00:48,120
which is a very big, strong base model,

16
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:51,040
is ultimately a little bit limited.

17
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:55,000
When we went from GPT-3 to something like ChatGPT,

18
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,480
I'm sure if your first exposure to these systems

19
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,080
was ChatGPT was kind of amazing, if you

20
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:02,580
go back and interact with GPT-3, you'll be like,

21
00:01:02,580 --> 00:01:03,380
what is this thing?

22
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:04,480
What is the point of this thing?

23
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,397
And the only thing you could really do with it

24
00:01:06,397 --> 00:01:10,070
was things like copywriting or these fun little tasks,

25
00:01:10,070 --> 00:01:12,320
where you don't really need reliability or instruction

26
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:13,600
following.

27
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,520
So today, we are going to get from GPT-3 to something that

28
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:21,120
is getting basically close to ChatGPT.

29
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,480
So this is the very first part that we need.

30
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:29,000
And in the next lecture, we will go from ChatGPT to GPT o1

31
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,520
or any of the thinking models that we have today.

32
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:33,360
So those are going to be the two parts.

33
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:35,880
And the process that will take us from the left to the right

34
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:41,880
will be a process that usually people call post-training.

35
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:45,760
And I want to emphasize just how remarkable it is that we

36
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:47,720
can do instruction following.

37
00:01:47,720 --> 00:01:50,680
Instruction following is this process

38
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:53,600
of being able to put in a pretty long, complicated prompt

39
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:59,760
and get back very reasonable answers at the GPT-3 level,

40
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:01,520
fine-grained control of these systems

41
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:02,740
was basically impossible.

42
00:02:02,740 --> 00:02:04,823
Like you would put in a bunch of few-shot prompts,

43
00:02:04,823 --> 00:02:07,100
and you'd say, well, if my examples were good enough,

44
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:08,960
I'd get something reasonable.

45
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:12,240
In practice, your ability to steer these models is limited.

46
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:15,480
But once GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 come around,

47
00:02:15,480 --> 00:02:19,480
you start getting these examples of very long programmatic

48
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:20,020
prompts.

49
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:23,920
And then the model really one-shotting the whole prompt.

50
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:27,000
And so what do you really need in order

51
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,840
to get this kind of instruction following to work?

52
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:32,620
And so pre-training is always important.

53
00:02:32,620 --> 00:02:35,140
It's the basis on which we build all of this.

54
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:37,240
So if we ignore pre-training and we just

55
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:40,438
try to train our way to victory, we will get none of the things

56
00:02:40,438 --> 00:02:40,980
that we want.

57
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:42,320
Absolutely none of it.

58
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:44,640
So pre-training is critical because it

59
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,080
scales in this very diverse, broad way.

60
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,800
But once we have pre-training, we

61
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,920
have to then extract the kinds of behaviors

62
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:54,800
that we want out of this kind of primordial soup

63
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:55,900
that is pre-training.

64
00:02:55,900 --> 00:02:58,400
And to do that, we are going to have

65
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:00,840
to do much more explicit data collection, much more

66
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:03,880
explicit steering, and a lot of messy engineering

67
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:06,940
that in some ways we don't have to do so far.

68
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:09,420
I mean, the architecture stuff you might think is messy.

69
00:03:09,420 --> 00:03:11,680
I think it really doesn't compare to the messiness

70
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:14,280
of real world post-training.

71
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:16,660
And so we're going to go and collect some data,

72
00:03:16,660 --> 00:03:18,720
or we will talk as if we are going

73
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:20,280
to go and collect some data.

74
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:22,800
We're going to talk about what that data looks like,

75
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:28,720
how to train with it, and what the scale of that looks like.

76
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:31,000
And I will preface the rest of this lecture

77
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,520
and say, information about frontier post-training

78
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:37,980
is honestly pretty sparse in many, many ways.

79
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:40,320
If you look at the materials that I'm

80
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,520
going to reference today, a lot of it's

81
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:43,980
going to be actually pretty old.

82
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:46,600
It's going to be before the competition from ChatGPT

83
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:48,440
started to heat up.

84
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:51,080
So for example, the RLHF paper, learning

85
00:03:51,080 --> 00:03:53,000
to summarize from human feedback,

86
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:54,500
that's actually incredibly detailed.

87
00:03:54,500 --> 00:03:56,382
I'll point to that today in lecture.

88
00:03:56,382 --> 00:03:57,840
If you're interested, I suggest you

89
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:59,460
go read some of the appendices.

90
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:02,740
It contains things like detailed annotation guidelines,

91
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:06,480
how they were instructing people to give feedback to the models,

92
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:10,600
similarly with Anthropic's HH paper back in 2022.

93
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:13,480
And then now that competition has heated up, basically,

94
00:04:13,480 --> 00:04:15,400
none of the vendors want to release

95
00:04:15,400 --> 00:04:18,519
any information about their post-training processes.

96
00:04:18,519 --> 00:04:21,279
The data is very much a trade secret.

97
00:04:21,279 --> 00:04:23,840
And so we are not going to be able to talk about annotation

98
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:26,520
guidelines and things like that for anything that resembles

99
00:04:26,520 --> 00:04:29,960
a modern language model.

100
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:34,200
We do have closed source-- or open source models.

101
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:36,960
But on the other hand, a lot of the open source recipes

102
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:38,380
rely on distillation.

103
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:42,640
I think its nature is very different from what the frontier

104
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:45,760
labs are doing in terms of human data collection, which is really

105
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:47,660
a very tricky and messy thing.

106
00:04:47,660 --> 00:04:49,648
And I'll try to point out some of that,

107
00:04:49,648 --> 00:04:51,440
but it will be difficult to talk about some

108
00:04:51,440 --> 00:04:54,580
of that very comprehensively.

109
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:55,080
OK.

110
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:58,160
And then, just as an example of this,

111
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:01,800
I was doing some pre-research for this lecture earlier,

112
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:04,880
and I found this very fun example back in 2023

113
00:05:04,880 --> 00:05:08,440
about the trade secretness of post-training data, where

114
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:11,080
I guess, Scale AI's internal documents got

115
00:05:11,080 --> 00:05:12,430
leaked at some point.

116
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:14,680
And they had a whole bunch of very frank conversations

117
00:05:14,680 --> 00:05:17,100
about what was happening in post-training data land.

118
00:05:17,100 --> 00:05:18,600
And one of them was like, well, they

119
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:20,580
were trying to make Google Bard better,

120
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:24,380
but they couldn't figure out why GPT-4 was so much better.

121
00:05:24,380 --> 00:05:27,320
And so they said, well, we need to reverse engineer what's

122
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:29,600
happening, and we need to get our annotators

123
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:33,160
to produce responses that are more detailed and better

124
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:34,140
than GPT-4.

125
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:36,455
So these kinds of competitive dynamics

126
00:05:36,455 --> 00:05:37,580
are happening all the time.

127
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:39,520
And that's also why some of this detail

128
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:42,960
is going to be missing from today's lecture.

129
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:46,320
That said, algorithm-wise, we do have a pretty good handle

130
00:05:46,320 --> 00:05:47,480
on what is happening.

131
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:49,902
But algorithms are not really the secret sauce.

132
00:05:49,902 --> 00:05:51,860
It's not where a lot of the leverage is.

133
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:53,520
It's going to be the data.

134
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:58,020
So if we look at the RLHF papers,

135
00:05:58,020 --> 00:06:00,900
we see a very straightforward recipe, two-part recipe.

136
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:04,480
The first part is you go and collect some demonstration data.

137
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:08,160
So for every prompt you want some annotator

138
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:10,260
to provide supervised fine-tuning data,

139
00:06:10,260 --> 00:06:13,020
so reference responses for that prompt.

140
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,760
And you're going to do some fine-tuning.

141
00:06:14,760 --> 00:06:16,343
Then after you have that, you're going

142
00:06:16,343 --> 00:06:18,280
to do some reinforcement learning of some kind

143
00:06:18,280 --> 00:06:21,640
to try to shape the behavior of the model to be more aligned

144
00:06:21,640 --> 00:06:25,820
or more in line with what humans think are good responses.

145
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:29,020
So I'm going to start by talking about this very first phase,

146
00:06:29,020 --> 00:06:32,920
which is the SFT part of post-training.

147
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:35,960
And this is the part that's really basically all about data.

148
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,640
We all know how to SFT a model.

149
00:06:37,640 --> 00:06:40,140
That's basically exactly the same as pre-training.

150
00:06:40,140 --> 00:06:43,720
So the only real difference, with some minor variation,

151
00:06:43,720 --> 00:06:45,620
is going to be the training data.

152
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:47,640
So I will focus on the training data

153
00:06:47,640 --> 00:06:52,520
almost entirely for this part of the lecture.

154
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:53,680
OK.

155
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:58,520
With a little bit of discussion about the method.

156
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,700
So we will go through the data.

157
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:03,660
We are going to look at some examples.

158
00:07:03,660 --> 00:07:06,080
I'll talk about some historical developments

159
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:08,400
and what the trends are, at least in open source

160
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:10,560
post-training data land.

161
00:07:10,560 --> 00:07:12,460
And then I will talk about some pitfalls,

162
00:07:12,460 --> 00:07:14,760
some ways in which collecting this kind of data

163
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:17,260
is much harder than you think.

164
00:07:17,260 --> 00:07:20,880
And if you've taken classes like 224N,

165
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:23,960
you're going to find some things that are very reminiscent

166
00:07:23,960 --> 00:07:27,800
of issues that you see when you start building classic

167
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,960
supervised deep learning models from scratch.

168
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:34,200
So let me take a moment to pause,

169
00:07:34,200 --> 00:07:37,480
and then just talk about the progression of SFT data

170
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:40,160
over the years.

171
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:43,060
I think I'm going to go top left to bottom right.

172
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:44,980
That's roughly my chronological ordering.

173
00:07:44,980 --> 00:07:46,730
And there's been a lot of different tricks

174
00:07:46,730 --> 00:07:49,840
and a lot of things that people have done in the open source

175
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:53,800
language modeling land to try to both improve system performance

176
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:58,240
and to try to understand what factors of this kind of data

177
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:01,140
are useful to train models.

178
00:08:01,140 --> 00:08:04,320
I think, really, the oldest and in some ways

179
00:08:04,320 --> 00:08:07,760
the most visionary of this is things like FLAN.

180
00:08:07,760 --> 00:08:11,240
This was used to train the T5 model by Google.

181
00:08:11,240 --> 00:08:13,900
The rough idea for this was to say, well,

182
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:15,640
NLP people have already collected

183
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:18,780
a bunch of supervised data sets of inputs and outputs.

184
00:08:18,780 --> 00:08:21,800
We should just train the model on all of these.

185
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:24,600
Reasonable thing to try.

186
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:26,240
Turned out, for various reasons, this

187
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:27,490
was not the right thing to do.

188
00:08:27,490 --> 00:08:29,280
I'll talk about that later.

189
00:08:29,280 --> 00:08:32,960
Self-instruct, and other follow ups to that, this was also

190
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:36,460
very forward-looking in terms of its thinking was saying, well,

191
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:39,419
why can't we use the model itself to generate data?

192
00:08:39,419 --> 00:08:41,400
Well, models are getting better all the time.

193
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:43,400
And in fact, they might even be better than some

194
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:44,580
of our annotators.

195
00:08:44,580 --> 00:08:49,840
Why don't we get models to write very good, high quality

196
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:51,180
responses to our inputs?

197
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:53,120
And that's the self-instruct idea.

198
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:56,680
And then there came a bunch of distillation style approaches.

199
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:58,520
Alpaca is one of them.

200
00:08:58,520 --> 00:08:59,960
The Berkeley folks had one called

201
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:04,560
Vicuna in which they were using online user shared

202
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:09,400
prompts as the way to-- as the inputs to distill on.

203
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:11,040
And then there were other efforts

204
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:14,020
that were more entirely human-driven, like Open Assistant.

205
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:15,972
You can think of this kind of like Wikipedia.

206
00:09:15,972 --> 00:09:17,680
It was a bunch of people getting together

207
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:20,360
to write prompts and responses.

208
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:23,200
And then you have a new generation WizardLM

209
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:25,660
and Tulu3 and others of saying, well,

210
00:09:25,660 --> 00:09:29,180
that language models are very good synthetic data generators.

211
00:09:29,180 --> 00:09:31,960
Why don't we come up with increasingly complicated ways

212
00:09:31,960 --> 00:09:34,240
of generating instruction following

213
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:35,700
data using language models?

214
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:38,720
And then finally, I'll touch briefly on this.

215
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:45,080
SFT and post-training has changed yet again in recent years because I think the focus is less on chat,

216
00:09:45,080 --> 00:09:47,340
and much more on agents and tool use.

217
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:50,920
And so there is now a new generation of SFT pipelines

218
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:54,200
that are designed to generate tool use and agentic data.

219
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:58,280
So this is roughly a bunch of different developments

220
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:02,560
in post-training data land, at least in the open source world.

221
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:04,560
So in the closed source world, where--

222
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:07,220
well, some of the companies may be distilling each other,

223
00:10:07,220 --> 00:10:09,260
but where you're also collecting human data.

224
00:10:09,260 --> 00:10:10,760
How you're collecting the human data

225
00:10:10,760 --> 00:10:12,010
is a big important ingredient.

226
00:10:12,010 --> 00:10:15,200
That's not reflected in any of this discussion.

227
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:15,700
OK.

228
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:19,240
I can pause here in case anyone has questions

229
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:20,880
before I move on to what we're going

230
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:25,480
to do next is look at some of the details of these data sets.

231
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:26,040
Good.

232
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:27,120
Oh, yeah.

233
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:30,420
So for post-training, we need the input-output pairs, right?

234
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:31,020
That's right.

235
00:10:31,020 --> 00:10:32,295
So input-output pairs.

236
00:10:32,295 --> 00:10:34,420
Does the correctness of those input-output pairs matter?

237
00:10:34,420 --> 00:10:36,080
How far does it--Yeah.

238
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:37,480
So the question was how much does

239
00:10:37,480 --> 00:10:40,240
the correctness of the input-output pairs matter?

240
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:41,620
That's a nuanced question.

241
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:44,360
I mean, I think the top level answer

242
00:10:44,360 --> 00:10:47,680
is you should go collect the highest quality responses you

243
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:50,920
can because the bad stuff will teach the model to do bad stuff.

244
00:10:50,920 --> 00:10:54,200
On the other hand, it turns out that you can SFT models

245
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:56,400
on all sorts of strange things, and they will

246
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,720
learn to follow instructions.

247
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:02,200
I think Percy's former student had a paper in which you're

248
00:11:02,200 --> 00:11:06,500
like training models without even the responses or something.

249
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:09,580
And you can train models to be instruction following.

250
00:11:09,580 --> 00:11:12,240
And so there's a lot of pre-training generalization

251
00:11:12,240 --> 00:11:14,580
behaviors that let you get away with worse quality data.

252
00:11:14,580 --> 00:11:17,160
So it's actually a pretty nuanced question,

253
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:21,680
what you need in instruction following data.

254
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:22,220
OK.

255
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:24,240
Good.

256
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:28,180
So I want to maybe start with FLAN.

257
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:32,920
I think FLAN is very much like a forward-looking data set

258
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:33,740
for its time.

259
00:11:33,740 --> 00:11:37,540
It's established this idea of doing this multitask

260
00:11:37,540 --> 00:11:38,480
post-training.

261
00:11:38,480 --> 00:11:40,140
If you're going to do downstream tasks,

262
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:42,980
go collect all the downstream tasks and train on it.

263
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:45,680
Very reasonable, but smart idea at the time.

264
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:47,940
But if we look at what's in FLAN,

265
00:11:47,940 --> 00:11:50,400
we actually find that it's a little bit

266
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:52,960
of a strange data set.

267
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:56,592
You have this thing that's like, well, here's an email.

268
00:11:56,592 --> 00:11:58,800
And then there's this thing that gets appended, which

269
00:11:58,800 --> 00:12:01,120
is write a subject line for this email,

270
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:03,880
and then the subject line goes on the right.

271
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:05,340
This is a very strange thing.

272
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:07,600
I don't think you've ever probably prompted

273
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:10,880
a model to do a task like this, let alone the instructions

274
00:12:10,880 --> 00:12:11,820
at the very end.

275
00:12:11,820 --> 00:12:13,560
And the reason why you get something

276
00:12:13,560 --> 00:12:16,840
like this is because FLAN is generated from existing data

277
00:12:16,840 --> 00:12:17,340
sets.

278
00:12:17,340 --> 00:12:20,020
So there's a data set called Enron emails.

279
00:12:20,020 --> 00:12:22,280
This is basically a way to turn Enron emails

280
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:25,500
into a supervised task because it has a body, and a subject,

281
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:28,760
You can make this subject prediction task.

282
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:32,180
Similarly, you have a summarization task.

283
00:12:32,180 --> 00:12:34,760
This is—I don't know if this was taken from CNN, Daily Mail,

284
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:36,218
or Exxon, but it was taken from one

285
00:12:36,218 --> 00:12:39,880
of the big, major summarization datasets.

286
00:12:39,880 --> 00:12:42,360
Highlights for this article, take the article part

287
00:12:42,360 --> 00:12:45,820
of a summarization dataset, put the summary on the right.

288
00:12:45,820 --> 00:12:47,840
But you immediately notice, compared

289
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:51,400
to something like ChatGPT, the summaries are very short.

290
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:53,180
In fact, if you look really closely,

291
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:54,820
the summaries are often hallucinated.

292
00:12:54,820 --> 00:12:58,120
There's a bunch of details that are not in the inputs.

293
00:12:58,120 --> 00:13:00,780
And in some ways, related to this question,

294
00:13:00,780 --> 00:13:04,640
the original NLP datasets, from which FLAN was built,

295
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:07,840
was not the highest of quality.

296
00:13:07,840 --> 00:13:09,460
And so when you train on this thing,

297
00:13:09,460 --> 00:13:12,360
you also inherit a whole bunch of deficiencies from those data

298
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:13,080
sets.

299
00:13:13,080 --> 00:13:15,360
And the unnaturalness of the structure of these data

300
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,760
sets also carry down all the way.

301
00:13:18,760 --> 00:13:21,100
And so this is a very smart idea.

302
00:13:21,100 --> 00:13:23,840
FLAN is a very smart idea to create this big dataset out

303
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:28,760
of these dataset—existing benchmarks.

304
00:13:28,760 --> 00:13:31,900
But really, the unnaturalness really gets you.

305
00:13:31,900 --> 00:13:34,380
And I'll mention one more thing, going back one slide.

306
00:13:34,380 --> 00:13:37,060
I think when FLAN was originally constructed,

307
00:13:37,060 --> 00:13:40,290
part of the theory of how post-training should work

308
00:13:40,290 --> 00:13:41,970
was that you needed scale.

309
00:13:41,970 --> 00:13:44,270
In pre-training, we know we need a lot of scale.

310
00:13:44,270 --> 00:13:46,450
So you might also think that in post-training, we

311
00:13:46,450 --> 00:13:49,590
need a ton of scale to unlock capabilities.

312
00:13:49,590 --> 00:13:52,090
And so because of that, they took tons of datasets and lots

313
00:13:52,090 --> 00:13:54,890
of data, and had this gigantic dataset.

314
00:13:54,890 --> 00:13:57,730
We'll see later on that a lot of these datasets

315
00:13:57,730 --> 00:14:00,430
just get really much smaller, and it still works.

316
00:14:00,430 --> 00:14:03,450
And so really, part of the change from FLAN to later data

317
00:14:03,450 --> 00:14:07,090
sets is this observation that if you have a sufficiently strong,

318
00:14:07,090 --> 00:14:09,490
big, pre-trained model, you can actually get away

319
00:14:09,490 --> 00:14:12,250
with very few high quality examples because

320
00:14:12,250 --> 00:14:14,552
of pre-training generalization is going to get you

321
00:14:14,552 --> 00:14:15,510
quite a bit of the way.

322
00:14:15,510 --> 00:14:19,090
And so it's the wrong point in some ways on the quality,

323
00:14:19,090 --> 00:14:20,430
quantity trade-off.

324
00:14:20,430 --> 00:14:24,210
Although, there was no way to until we explored the full space

325
00:14:24,210 --> 00:14:26,770
of these data sets.

326
00:14:26,770 --> 00:14:31,650
And then I think, after that, maybe next point in how

327
00:14:31,650 --> 00:14:35,730
instruction tuning data sets are understanding of it changed

328
00:14:35,730 --> 00:14:40,112
was after ChatGPT came out, some of my students

329
00:14:40,112 --> 00:14:41,570
did this thing called Alpaca, where

330
00:14:41,570 --> 00:14:44,870
they distilled ChatGPT traces to get input-output pairs.

331
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:46,990
So this is an example from that data set.

332
00:14:46,990 --> 00:14:52,010
You get more natural-looking inputs, longer chatty outputs.

333
00:14:52,010 --> 00:14:55,610
Because of course, it's taken from ChatGPT.

334
00:14:55,610 --> 00:14:59,570
And what we found was that these kinds of examples, now,

335
00:14:59,570 --> 00:15:05,090
reliably induced ChatGPT-like behavior on models.

336
00:15:05,090 --> 00:15:10,810
Importantly, only when we did it to the original Llama models,

337
00:15:10,810 --> 00:15:13,890
so pre-training, post-training, both of them need to work out.

338
00:15:13,890 --> 00:15:16,902
But we did find that this was the point at which

339
00:15:16,902 --> 00:15:18,110
these things started to work.

340
00:15:18,110 --> 00:15:20,090
And so I think people realized at this point

341
00:15:20,090 --> 00:15:23,610
that if you got this kind of chat-style data,

342
00:15:23,610 --> 00:15:28,710
it was actually not that hard to build ChatGPT-style systems.

343
00:15:28,710 --> 00:15:30,710
Getting the details right is still hard,

344
00:15:30,710 --> 00:15:33,250
but to get most of the way.

345
00:15:33,250 --> 00:15:37,810
So then I think there was this big push to create, essentially,

346
00:15:37,810 --> 00:15:41,450
the highest quality human-driven version of these kinds of data

347
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:42,810
sets.

348
00:15:42,810 --> 00:15:45,190
To give you a little bit more context, at the time,

349
00:15:45,190 --> 00:15:48,110
I think after ChatGPT came out and Alpaca came out,

350
00:15:48,110 --> 00:15:50,250
I think there was this enormous optimism

351
00:15:50,250 --> 00:15:54,170
that if only we could collect a sufficiently high quality

352
00:15:54,170 --> 00:15:57,150
and large, instruction-tuning data set,

353
00:15:57,150 --> 00:15:59,290
we could then catch up and match a lot

354
00:15:59,290 --> 00:16:01,510
of the performance of these big closed source labs.

355
00:16:01,510 --> 00:16:04,103
So there's a lot of energy and excitement

356
00:16:04,103 --> 00:16:05,270
to building these data sets.

357
00:16:05,270 --> 00:16:07,530
And so in that context, Open Assistant

358
00:16:07,530 --> 00:16:11,290
is this big crowdsourced effort to build such a data set.

359
00:16:11,290 --> 00:16:12,810
So a lot of volunteers got together

360
00:16:12,810 --> 00:16:15,130
and they said, well, we're going to come up with really

361
00:16:15,130 --> 00:16:16,750
hard and interesting prompts.

362
00:16:16,750 --> 00:16:19,150
We're going to come up with good, high quality responses,

363
00:16:19,150 --> 00:16:20,567
and we're going to do it at scale.

364
00:16:20,567 --> 00:16:22,290
In the same way that Wikipedia managed

365
00:16:22,290 --> 00:16:24,850
to produce something very high quality at scale.

366
00:16:24,850 --> 00:16:27,410
A very admirable, very impressive effort

367
00:16:27,410 --> 00:16:29,470
that generated actually a decent amount of data.

368
00:16:29,470 --> 00:16:33,970
I forget if it's like 10,000 or more examples before stalling

369
00:16:33,970 --> 00:16:36,190
out as a project.

370
00:16:36,190 --> 00:16:39,290
I can maybe talk about that if someone's interested.

371
00:16:39,290 --> 00:16:42,830
But if you look now, we have chat-style inputs, very long,

372
00:16:42,830 --> 00:16:46,330
detailed, high quality expert responses on the right.

373
00:16:46,330 --> 00:16:47,690
Good stuff.

374
00:16:47,690 --> 00:16:49,870
I'll talk about some of the pitfalls later as well.

375
00:16:49,870 --> 00:16:50,370
OK.

376
00:16:50,370 --> 00:16:52,490
Finally, I've taken this from some

377
00:16:52,490 --> 00:16:55,470
of the newest of the instruction-tuning data sets.

378
00:16:55,470 --> 00:16:57,370
We've really started to move as a field,

379
00:16:57,370 --> 00:16:59,930
or in terms of the products that people want,

380
00:16:59,930 --> 00:17:02,290
from just a chat interface to something that's

381
00:17:02,290 --> 00:17:04,609
like a full agent system.

382
00:17:04,609 --> 00:17:06,670
So we don't want just textual responses.

383
00:17:06,670 --> 00:17:07,750
We want tool calls.

384
00:17:07,750 --> 00:17:10,349
We want to-do lists.

385
00:17:10,349 --> 00:17:12,303
If you've ever used Cloud Code or Codex,

386
00:17:12,303 --> 00:17:14,410
you know that it produces a to-do list

387
00:17:14,410 --> 00:17:16,010
to check off as it goes.

388
00:17:16,010 --> 00:17:17,390
We want all of those things.

389
00:17:17,390 --> 00:17:19,930
And so SFT data now, if you look at, for example

390
00:17:19,930 --> 00:17:22,349
Nemotron, so that's NVIDIA's open source efforts,

391
00:17:22,349 --> 00:17:24,890
their SFT data, a big chunk of it

392
00:17:24,890 --> 00:17:28,490
is these kinds of agentic SFT examples,

393
00:17:28,490 --> 00:17:31,730
where not only do you have the responses, which are provided

394
00:17:31,730 --> 00:17:36,610
in the assistant field here, but also tool calls that can happen

395
00:17:36,610 --> 00:17:38,430
in parallel alongside the text.

396
00:17:38,430 --> 00:17:40,390
And this is SFT data.

397
00:17:40,390 --> 00:17:45,450
So this is explicitly supervised into the model.

398
00:17:45,450 --> 00:17:47,450
And so now, we're moving much more

399
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:51,530
towards these structured formats.

400
00:17:51,530 --> 00:17:54,150
And I've been talking about this as I've gone through.

401
00:17:54,150 --> 00:17:57,370
But we've seen big, higher level transitions

402
00:17:57,370 --> 00:18:00,010
in how we think about and build these kinds of data sets.

403
00:18:00,010 --> 00:18:04,090
It's useful to roughly internalize these perspectives.

404
00:18:04,090 --> 00:18:07,210
One of them is this chattiness thing.

405
00:18:07,210 --> 00:18:09,970
It's hard for me to fully articulate what this is,

406
00:18:09,970 --> 00:18:13,050
but there is a sense in which the classic NLP data sets, even

407
00:18:13,050 --> 00:18:17,190
the high quality ones, were very input to programmatic output

408
00:18:17,190 --> 00:18:18,690
almost.

409
00:18:18,690 --> 00:18:21,270
But people don't really want to talk to NLP benchmark,

410
00:18:21,270 --> 00:18:22,590
they want to talk to people.

411
00:18:22,590 --> 00:18:24,090
And so there was this big shift that

412
00:18:24,090 --> 00:18:27,890
has happened towards more detailed human-like responses.

413
00:18:27,890 --> 00:18:30,530
There's a big shift towards higher quality annotators,

414
00:18:30,530 --> 00:18:31,650
and much more detailed.

415
00:18:31,650 --> 00:18:33,930
Open Assistant is a good example of this, of having

416
00:18:33,930 --> 00:18:35,610
experts write your responses.

417
00:18:35,610 --> 00:18:40,090
And then finally, tool use and figuring out the right interface

418
00:18:40,090 --> 00:18:43,193
and API for all of this has been a big final shift

419
00:18:43,193 --> 00:18:44,110
that's been happening.

420
00:18:44,110 --> 00:18:45,670
I'm sure things will keep changing,

421
00:18:45,670 --> 00:18:49,050
but these are some high level factors

422
00:18:49,050 --> 00:18:52,930
to think about in terms of how SFT data has changed

423
00:18:52,930 --> 00:18:54,830
over the last few years.

424
00:18:54,830 --> 00:18:55,330
OK.

425
00:18:55,330 --> 00:19:01,450
I'll talk about all sorts of pitfalls or challenges

426
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:03,330
in a moment.

427
00:19:03,330 --> 00:19:05,970
Any questions on the specifics of data sets and things

428
00:19:05,970 --> 00:19:08,230
like this?

429
00:19:08,230 --> 00:19:08,730
Good.

430
00:19:08,730 --> 00:19:10,210
OK.

431
00:19:10,210 --> 00:19:11,930
So now, I think, let's say, you are

432
00:19:11,930 --> 00:19:16,850
put in charge of collecting human annotation data for SFT.

433
00:19:16,850 --> 00:19:19,910
What kinds of things should you be mindful of?

434
00:19:19,910 --> 00:19:22,610
There's lots of formatting things

435
00:19:22,610 --> 00:19:24,470
that you might want to think about.

436
00:19:24,470 --> 00:19:26,290
There's also things like knowledge,

437
00:19:26,290 --> 00:19:29,010
how much knowledge should be put into the responses?

438
00:19:29,010 --> 00:19:31,510
How much data points do you want?

439
00:19:31,510 --> 00:19:35,390
And how do you deal with safety of your model?

440
00:19:35,390 --> 00:19:39,530
What kinds of safety data should you be collecting?

441
00:19:39,530 --> 00:19:43,510
And I think one thing I want to highlight is length is--

442
00:19:43,510 --> 00:19:47,130
and style variation, in general, is a very, very big part

443
00:19:47,130 --> 00:19:48,910
of this post-training process.

444
00:19:48,910 --> 00:19:53,490
People say, Claude has a different tone than ChatGPT.

445
00:19:53,490 --> 00:19:56,130
ChatGPT is too chatty, so on and so forth.

446
00:19:56,130 --> 00:19:57,930
All of these are conscious decisions

447
00:19:57,930 --> 00:20:00,250
that are made by the data collection folks.

448
00:20:00,250 --> 00:20:02,510
And also, we see very wide variation

449
00:20:02,510 --> 00:20:04,130
if you look at the data sets in terms

450
00:20:04,130 --> 00:20:06,850
of the length of the responses.

451
00:20:06,850 --> 00:20:09,290
And one thing I do want to highlight

452
00:20:09,290 --> 00:20:13,730
is when you go and evaluate preferences,

453
00:20:13,730 --> 00:20:16,810
these stylistic factors matter a ton.

454
00:20:16,810 --> 00:20:19,490
So people will get very easily tricked.

455
00:20:19,490 --> 00:20:21,250
I don't know if tricked is the right word.

456
00:20:21,250 --> 00:20:24,130
They will very often select responses

457
00:20:24,130 --> 00:20:26,850
that have bullet-pointed lists in their outputs,

458
00:20:26,850 --> 00:20:28,970
or they will select responses that

459
00:20:28,970 --> 00:20:30,870
have more and longer detail.

460
00:20:30,870 --> 00:20:33,313
I think part of this is very natural, especially

461
00:20:33,313 --> 00:20:35,730
if you're in an evaluation setting, where you put in front

462
00:20:35,730 --> 00:20:38,390
of two examples and someone asks you which one is better,

463
00:20:38,390 --> 00:20:41,330
I think more detailed and list-like responses

464
00:20:41,330 --> 00:20:42,850
are generally good.

465
00:20:42,850 --> 00:20:45,730
But this does induce very explicit distortions

466
00:20:45,730 --> 00:20:50,630
in the kinds of tone that a chatbot has relative to people.

467
00:20:53,650 --> 00:20:55,250
These factors-- oh, yeah.

468
00:20:55,250 --> 00:20:57,930
So [INAUDIBLE] complex matter in the data set?

469
00:20:57,930 --> 00:21:01,055
I'll talk about that later.

470
00:21:01,055 --> 00:21:02,930
And you can ask me questions if I don't fully

471
00:21:02,930 --> 00:21:04,610
answer your question then.

472
00:21:07,750 --> 00:21:09,710
But I think the thing about style,

473
00:21:09,710 --> 00:21:11,690
and the thing to be careful about,

474
00:21:11,690 --> 00:21:13,990
is if you're looking at engagement signals,

475
00:21:13,990 --> 00:21:17,130
as most of these companies are, it's very, very easy

476
00:21:17,130 --> 00:21:20,410
to fool yourself into thinking you're getting better data,

477
00:21:20,410 --> 00:21:23,990
when in reality, your model's capabilities are not changing.

478
00:21:23,990 --> 00:21:26,010
So if you look at all of these different kinds

479
00:21:26,010 --> 00:21:30,290
of post-training data sets that you can train on,

480
00:21:30,290 --> 00:21:33,420
you'll see big variations in preferences when you

481
00:21:33,420 --> 00:21:34,670
train on some of these things.

482
00:21:34,670 --> 00:21:38,073
So that's the right column over there, AlpacaEval, for example.

483
00:21:38,073 --> 00:21:39,490
But really, it doesn't necessarily

484
00:21:39,490 --> 00:21:42,702
change standard benchmarking evaluations.

485
00:21:42,702 --> 00:21:44,410
So your models aren't necessarily smarter

486
00:21:44,410 --> 00:21:46,035
because you've trained on certain kinds

487
00:21:46,035 --> 00:21:48,410
of post-training data, at least in these ones.

488
00:21:48,410 --> 00:21:51,650
But you can very much shift the engagement signals.

489
00:21:51,650 --> 00:21:54,050
And so this is telling you, you want

490
00:21:54,050 --> 00:21:56,410
to think about style control separately

491
00:21:56,410 --> 00:21:58,750
from capabilities control.

492
00:21:58,750 --> 00:21:59,250
OK.

493
00:21:59,250 --> 00:22:00,890
The other thing, and this, I think,

494
00:22:00,890 --> 00:22:04,330
is a quite, in many ways, important thing

495
00:22:04,330 --> 00:22:06,610
to think about in terms of how we collect and think

496
00:22:06,610 --> 00:22:08,610
about post-training data.

497
00:22:08,610 --> 00:22:10,710
I think Open Assistant is very instructive

498
00:22:10,710 --> 00:22:13,610
because it was an effort that was intended

499
00:22:13,610 --> 00:22:17,890
to be the highest quality in some ways, post-training data.

500
00:22:17,890 --> 00:22:19,810
And so you often see things like this.

501
00:22:19,810 --> 00:22:22,430
Like for example, this reference-- sorry,

502
00:22:22,430 --> 00:22:25,730
this response contains a reference to an article.

503
00:22:25,730 --> 00:22:30,450
This J Michel, the pay of corporate executives, and blah, blah.

504
00:22:30,450 --> 00:22:34,050
And so this is actually teaching the model two different things

505
00:22:34,050 --> 00:22:34,550
at once.

506
00:22:34,550 --> 00:22:37,610
Let's say, you're SFT-ing on this data.

507
00:22:37,610 --> 00:22:40,350
So first of all, you're doing next token prediction.

508
00:22:40,350 --> 00:22:44,610
So it's teaching the model about this Bivens J & Michelle L

509
00:22:44,610 --> 00:22:46,250
citation.

510
00:22:46,250 --> 00:22:48,410
It's also telling the model another thing, which

511
00:22:48,410 --> 00:22:51,490
is when you're giving a response, a good thing to do

512
00:22:51,490 --> 00:22:52,950
is to give a reference.

513
00:22:52,950 --> 00:22:55,770
So these are two different mechanisms

514
00:22:55,770 --> 00:22:57,607
that are going on at once.

515
00:22:57,607 --> 00:23:00,190
You're teaching the model about different pieces of knowledge,

516
00:23:00,190 --> 00:23:03,330
which is good because you want the model to be knowledgeable,

517
00:23:03,330 --> 00:23:04,810
but you're also teaching the model

518
00:23:04,810 --> 00:23:07,730
to emit detailed knowledge.

519
00:23:07,730 --> 00:23:10,370
And this is sometimes problematic

520
00:23:10,370 --> 00:23:12,770
because what this is going to force the model to do

521
00:23:12,770 --> 00:23:14,670
is sometimes it will misgeneralize,

522
00:23:14,670 --> 00:23:17,330
and it will hallucinate out a reference instead of actually

523
00:23:17,330 --> 00:23:18,790
giving a proper reference.

524
00:23:18,790 --> 00:23:20,850
Models don't really know necessarily

525
00:23:20,850 --> 00:23:24,930
whether a reference is true or false.

526
00:23:24,930 --> 00:23:27,290
And there's been a lot of interesting arguments

527
00:23:27,290 --> 00:23:30,650
that people have made around this.

528
00:23:30,650 --> 00:23:33,710
And I think the folklore, which I think is not wrong.

529
00:23:33,710 --> 00:23:35,410
There's some good empirical evidence

530
00:23:35,410 --> 00:23:37,090
to support this folklore.

531
00:23:37,090 --> 00:23:40,610
Is that if you train a model, especially at the SFT phase,

532
00:23:40,610 --> 00:23:47,010
to emit facts that it does not, this will make it hallucinate.

533
00:23:47,010 --> 00:23:48,690
Because the model, as I said, is trying

534
00:23:48,690 --> 00:23:50,430
to generalize two behaviors at once.

535
00:23:50,430 --> 00:23:53,450
It's trying to generalize the knowledge but also the format.

536
00:23:53,450 --> 00:23:55,010
And so teaching it something where

537
00:23:55,010 --> 00:23:57,610
you have the format in a piece of unknown knowledge

538
00:23:57,610 --> 00:24:01,770
is kind of teaching the model to forcibly emit unknown knowledge.

539
00:24:01,770 --> 00:24:06,810
And people have shown that if you train on, for example,

540
00:24:06,810 --> 00:24:08,570
information that you don't really know,

541
00:24:08,570 --> 00:24:12,930
then this leads to hallucination and overfitting

542
00:24:12,930 --> 00:24:14,232
to pieces of knowledge.

543
00:24:14,232 --> 00:24:16,190
Whereas if you're training on only known facts,

544
00:24:16,190 --> 00:24:18,770
you don't necessarily see this.

545
00:24:18,770 --> 00:24:21,032
John Schulman makes a separate argument,

546
00:24:21,032 --> 00:24:23,490
which is that this is the reason why you need reinforcement

547
00:24:23,490 --> 00:24:24,190
learning and training.

548
00:24:24,190 --> 00:24:25,610
I mean, this is unsurprising because if you

549
00:24:25,610 --> 00:24:28,290
know John Schulman, he's like a reinforcement learning guy.

550
00:24:28,290 --> 00:24:29,370
He made PPO.

551
00:24:29,370 --> 00:24:32,110
Of course, he is very pro reinforcement learning.

552
00:24:32,110 --> 00:24:33,450
But the argument is sound.

553
00:24:33,450 --> 00:24:35,570
That, in some ways, in order to teach

554
00:24:35,570 --> 00:24:37,170
the model what it knows and doesn't

555
00:24:37,170 --> 00:24:39,690
do have to be policy-dependent.

556
00:24:39,690 --> 00:24:42,730
You can't have an external person shoving knowledge down

557
00:24:42,730 --> 00:24:45,690
your throat if you want the model to be calibrated about

558
00:24:45,690 --> 00:24:48,110
what it knows and doesn't.

559
00:24:48,110 --> 00:24:49,730
OK.

560
00:24:49,730 --> 00:24:53,210
And so to put that piece together,

561
00:24:53,210 --> 00:24:56,930
you might not want to train on the highest quality data

562
00:24:56,930 --> 00:24:59,070
if the model doesn't already have that data.

563
00:24:59,070 --> 00:25:00,810
Tail knowledge can actually sometimes

564
00:25:00,810 --> 00:25:04,270
be actively harmful in that it might induce hallucinations,

565
00:25:04,270 --> 00:25:06,490
especially if it's associated with markers

566
00:25:06,490 --> 00:25:07,950
like reference colon.

567
00:25:07,950 --> 00:25:11,490
They'll force the model to emit a reference afterwards.

568
00:25:11,490 --> 00:25:16,650
I'll talk about RLHF later, but this is one of the reasons why reinforcement learning is

569
00:25:16,650 --> 00:25:17,930
important and useful.

570
00:25:17,930 --> 00:25:19,470
For things that are model-dependent,

571
00:25:19,470 --> 00:25:22,770
knowing what you know, RL is a very useful way

572
00:25:22,770 --> 00:25:26,690
to teach models to not say things that it does not know.

573
00:25:26,690 --> 00:25:28,810
And so knowledge storage is actually

574
00:25:28,810 --> 00:25:31,410
very messy and a very nuanced concept.

575
00:25:31,410 --> 00:25:33,530
You don't really want to just say yes, just pack

576
00:25:33,530 --> 00:25:34,322
knowledge in there.

577
00:25:34,322 --> 00:25:36,753
You want to be very careful about exactly what supervision

578
00:25:36,753 --> 00:25:38,670
you're throwing in at the post-training stage.

579
00:25:38,670 --> 00:25:40,253
This is one of the reasons why I think

580
00:25:40,253 --> 00:25:41,850
post-training is quite messy.

581
00:25:41,850 --> 00:25:44,290
You have to deal with all of these realities of serving

582
00:25:44,290 --> 00:25:46,210
a system eventually to a user.

583
00:25:46,210 --> 00:25:47,370
Yes.

584
00:25:47,370 --> 00:25:49,830
What's the definition of tail knowledge?

585
00:25:49,830 --> 00:25:50,330
Yeah.

586
00:25:50,330 --> 00:25:53,110
I don't think there's a formal definition of knowledge.

587
00:25:53,110 --> 00:25:56,170
For example, in the past when I've worked on things like this,

588
00:25:56,170 --> 00:25:58,850
it's been things like the number of times

589
00:25:58,850 --> 00:26:03,070
that-- sorry, the length of a Wikipedia article, for example,

590
00:26:03,070 --> 00:26:06,193
might be a proxy for its well-knownness.

591
00:26:06,193 --> 00:26:07,610
And then you might say, yes, if we

592
00:26:07,610 --> 00:26:09,510
train on stuff that's not well known,

593
00:26:09,510 --> 00:26:11,330
we do see that the model hallucinates more.

594
00:26:11,330 --> 00:26:13,210
But there's no formal like, yes, this is tail

595
00:26:13,210 --> 00:26:15,210
and this is not knowledge, because knowledge

596
00:26:15,210 --> 00:26:18,810
itself can't really be pinned down, precisely.

597
00:26:18,810 --> 00:26:19,850
OK.

598
00:26:19,850 --> 00:26:20,350
Yeah.

599
00:26:27,690 --> 00:26:31,930
The question was why would RL help for hallucinations?

600
00:26:31,930 --> 00:26:33,590
And the reason why RL might help,

601
00:26:33,590 --> 00:26:36,330
let me give a folk story for why this might help.

602
00:26:36,330 --> 00:26:39,110
Imagine, the model has internal to it.

603
00:26:39,110 --> 00:26:42,890
I know something direction inside its activations.

604
00:26:42,890 --> 00:26:45,170
SFT time, maybe what's happening is

605
00:26:45,170 --> 00:26:48,770
you forced it to generate references from your SFT data.

606
00:26:48,770 --> 00:26:51,910
And so it's just going to generate references no matter what.

607
00:26:51,910 --> 00:26:54,210
But when you RL, you might notice

608
00:26:54,210 --> 00:26:57,830
that you get good rewards when you generate references in the I

609
00:26:57,830 --> 00:26:59,310
know stuff direction.

610
00:26:59,310 --> 00:27:02,410
And you get bad rewards when you generate references in the I

611
00:27:02,410 --> 00:27:03,550
don't know direction.

612
00:27:03,550 --> 00:27:06,530
And so then you might then learn to generalize that I know things

613
00:27:06,530 --> 00:27:10,410
or don't things activations into whether you emit a reference.

614
00:27:10,410 --> 00:27:12,210
So it's possible that if you know what

615
00:27:12,210 --> 00:27:15,250
somewhere inside the model, RL can help you extract that

616
00:27:15,250 --> 00:27:16,470
into your output policy.

617
00:27:16,470 --> 00:27:19,470
If you don't know what you know at all, at any level,

618
00:27:19,470 --> 00:27:21,050
RL cannot help you there.

619
00:27:21,050 --> 00:27:24,530
It can't help you with the problem of having

620
00:27:24,530 --> 00:27:26,430
some level of calibration.

621
00:27:26,430 --> 00:27:26,930
Yeah.

622
00:27:26,930 --> 00:27:28,230
So just to follow up on that.

623
00:27:28,230 --> 00:27:30,430
But if it emitted a bad reference,

624
00:27:30,430 --> 00:27:34,090
where do you get penalized in the loss function?

625
00:27:34,090 --> 00:27:36,770
In SFT, well, in some ways, implicitly, you're

626
00:27:36,770 --> 00:27:40,810
getting penalized for anything that is not a reference,

627
00:27:40,810 --> 00:27:42,810
like assigning any probability mass to something

628
00:27:42,810 --> 00:27:45,650
that is not this exact sequence is penalized.

629
00:27:45,650 --> 00:27:48,390
So in some ways, SFT isn't doing the wrong thing.

630
00:27:48,390 --> 00:27:50,210
This is a correct sequence.

631
00:27:50,210 --> 00:27:54,570
The problem is SFT is the generalization properties of this.

632
00:27:54,570 --> 00:27:58,050
That I can't perfectly generalize off this one example.

633
00:27:58,050 --> 00:28:00,330
There's two things, two disentangled things that

634
00:28:00,330 --> 00:28:03,270
are being taught here, one is the reference template,

635
00:28:03,270 --> 00:28:07,450
and then the other thing is the actual content of the knowledge.

636
00:28:07,450 --> 00:28:12,110
And if I generalize based on the template, I'm in big trouble.

637
00:28:12,110 --> 00:28:12,610
Great.

638
00:28:12,610 --> 00:28:13,470
Great questions.

639
00:28:13,470 --> 00:28:15,330
Yeah.

640
00:28:15,330 --> 00:28:16,690
OK.

641
00:28:16,690 --> 00:28:20,690
另外我想谈的是安全性。

642
00:28:20,690 --> 00:28:22,370
一旦我们完成预训练，我们

643
00:28:22,370 --> 00:28:24,250
就必须真正开始面对

644
00:28:24,250 --> 00:28:27,270
人们将如何使用它的种种复杂现实。

645
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:30,130
我认为预训练的人生活在象牙塔里，

646
00:28:30,130 --> 00:28:32,130
就像让我们压缩整个世界。

647
00:28:32,130 --> 00:28:34,950
但后训练的人必须说，是的。

648
00:28:34,950 --> 00:28:38,170
但如果人们在使用我们的系统

649
00:28:38,170 --> 00:28:41,090
进行政治操纵和虚假信息传播呢？

650
00:28:41,090 --> 00:28:42,950
后训练的人可能会说，是的，

651
00:28:42,950 --> 00:28:44,990
我们看到了 individualized spearfishing。

652
00:28:44,990 --> 00:28:46,650
我们需要防止这种情况发生。

653
00:28:46,650 --> 00:28:49,570
在某些方面，你是最后一道防线，

654
00:28:49,570 --> 00:28:51,910
在你和系统被滥用之间。

655
00:28:51,910 --> 00:28:52,970
那么你该怎么做？

656
00:28:52,970 --> 00:28:55,690
嗯，你必须对系统施加安全控制。

657
00:28:55,690 --> 00:28:59,110
它会拒绝对恶意输入作出回应。

658
00:28:59,110 --> 00:29:02,850
这是这些公司实际采用的

659
00:29:02,850 --> 00:29:03,830
执行安全控制的方式。

660
00:29:03,830 --> 00:29:07,250
所以这就落在了后训练团队的肩上。

661
00:29:07,250 --> 00:29:10,450
不幸的是，安全 SFT 信息

662
00:29:10,450 --> 00:29:14,330
似乎比能力相关的

663
00:29:14,330 --> 00:29:18,610
SFT 信息在公开文件中更为稀少。

664
00:29:18,610 --> 00:29:21,250
我提取了 Llama 2 的描述，

665
00:29:21,250 --> 00:29:22,890
关于他们如何做安全 SFT。

666
00:29:22,890 --> 00:29:25,730
如果说有什么不同，这是较为详细的描述之一。

667
00:29:25,730 --> 00:29:27,322
但你会看到他们甚至

668
00:29:27,322 --> 00:29:28,530
没有谈到用了多少示例。

669
00:29:28,530 --> 00:29:31,810
他们用于安全调优，这在我看来有点疯狂。

670
00:29:31,810 --> 00:29:34,010
但无论如何，实际上，所有

671
00:29:34,010 --> 00:29:36,610
安全调优方法运作的框架

672
00:29:36,610 --> 00:29:38,030
都是要平衡两件事。

673
00:29:38,030 --> 00:29:41,730
你想平衡我允许多少

674
00:29:41,730 --> 00:29:44,490
坏查询通过，我认为这就是违规

675
00:29:44,490 --> 00:29:45,610
率。

676
00:29:45,610 --> 00:29:48,040
然后你也不想过度拒绝，

677
00:29:48,040 --> 00:29:49,790
因为有人可能会问这样的问题，

678
00:29:49,790 --> 00:29:51,323
我如何杀死一个 Python 进程。

679
00:29:51,323 --> 00:29:52,990
然后如果你是一个机器人，它就会说，不，

680
00:29:52,990 --> 00:29:54,570
I can't let you kill anything, that's

681
00:29:54,570 --> 00:29:56,270
very frustrating to the user.

682
00:29:56,270 --> 00:29:58,470
And so the false refusal rate also needs to be low.

683
00:29:58,470 --> 00:30:01,450
So you want to control this and have a good Pareto

684
00:30:01,450 --> 00:30:04,290
trade-off between the two.

685
00:30:04,290 --> 00:30:07,170
And so what you do is you basically create

686
00:30:07,170 --> 00:30:11,170
various kinds of tailored data to navigate this trade-off.

687
00:30:11,170 --> 00:30:13,130
In terms of the scale of the amount of data

688
00:30:13,130 --> 00:30:16,850
you collect, you usually have something like a few thousand

689
00:30:16,850 --> 00:30:18,810
or tens of thousands of examples.

690
00:30:18,810 --> 00:30:20,610
For example, in Llama 2, I think it's

691
00:30:20,610 --> 00:30:25,388
about a few thousand examples that are used for this process.

692
00:30:25,388 --> 00:30:26,930
This state of affairs is a little bit

693
00:30:26,930 --> 00:30:29,590
unsatisfying if I left you with only that slide.

694
00:30:29,590 --> 00:30:32,930
So I will talk about it in a little bit more detail.

695
00:30:32,930 --> 00:30:37,250
I think if you want to look at how post-training is done,

696
00:30:37,250 --> 00:30:40,730
maybe the only good reference right now would be two or three.

697
00:30:40,730 --> 00:30:44,970
This would be the post-training pipeline for the OLMo models

698
00:30:44,970 --> 00:30:47,530
that Allen AI put out.

699
00:30:47,530 --> 00:30:50,490
And given the lack of details about post-training

700
00:30:50,490 --> 00:30:52,770
in many places, this is one of the few places where

701
00:30:52,770 --> 00:30:55,290
you can see a reasonably performant

702
00:30:55,290 --> 00:30:57,370
post-training pipeline in action.

703
00:30:57,370 --> 00:31:00,070
And they do have, for example, a safety component,

704
00:31:00,070 --> 00:31:05,570
which consists of something like 50,000-ish examples total.

705
00:31:05,570 --> 00:31:09,250
And if you look, they all follow the same fairly simple strategy

706
00:31:09,250 --> 00:31:12,890
for generating SFT data, which is the following.

707
00:31:12,890 --> 00:31:14,890
In a previous project, they had this thing

708
00:31:14,890 --> 00:31:19,250
called WildChat, where basically they gave free API or free chat

709
00:31:19,250 --> 00:31:21,210
access to a bunch of people so that they

710
00:31:21,210 --> 00:31:24,610
can go out and use various kinds of chat interactions.

711
00:31:24,610 --> 00:31:29,090
In exchange, they collected what they were doing on those chats.

712
00:31:29,090 --> 00:31:32,290
And from that, they were able to basically filter out

713
00:31:32,290 --> 00:31:36,170
a whole bunch of unsafe behaviors that were being attempted

714
00:31:36,170 --> 00:31:38,670
to be done on these chat interfaces,

715
00:31:38,670 --> 00:31:41,170
as well as jailbreaks that people attempted to do.

716
00:31:41,170 --> 00:31:42,850
And so they mined these at scale,

717
00:31:42,850 --> 00:31:45,830
and then created essentially the preferred response,

718
00:31:45,830 --> 00:31:48,450
which is to resist the jailbreak in one case,

719
00:31:48,450 --> 00:31:50,690
and to say no in another case.

720
00:31:50,690 --> 00:31:53,890
Fairly, very straightforward approach.

721
00:31:53,890 --> 00:31:58,330
And I think from what we see, at least in the technical reports

722
00:31:58,330 --> 00:32:01,260
and the model cards of the various closed source companies,

723
00:32:01,260 --> 00:32:03,010
similar things are happening, and that you

724
00:32:03,010 --> 00:32:06,950
look at your usage information, you find unsafe behaviors.

725
00:32:06,950 --> 00:32:10,010
You get your annotators to play whack-a-mole

726
00:32:10,010 --> 00:32:13,530
with these bad behaviors.

727
00:32:13,530 --> 00:32:14,050
OK.

728
00:32:14,050 --> 00:32:18,810
The final thing I'll mention, and this related to what I said about the number of examples

729
00:32:18,810 --> 00:32:20,090
that you need for SFT.

730
00:32:20,090 --> 00:32:23,170
I think one thing, if you haven't played with SFT before

731
00:32:23,170 --> 00:32:24,610
that you will be surprised by, is

732
00:32:24,610 --> 00:32:26,790
if you have a sufficiently capable model,

733
00:32:26,790 --> 00:32:30,210
it does not take very many examples to steer these systems.

734
00:32:30,210 --> 00:32:32,430
So if you want to do something safety tuning,

735
00:32:32,430 --> 00:32:35,210
if you take 500 examples, let's say,

736
00:32:35,210 --> 00:32:37,990
in the vein of you find a bunch of unsafe stuff,

737
00:32:37,990 --> 00:32:40,070
or you synthesize a bunch of unsafe stuff,

738
00:32:40,070 --> 00:32:43,550
and then you write refusals and you put that into the system,

739
00:32:43,550 --> 00:32:47,610
you'll find that even with as little as 500 examples, the rate

740
00:32:47,610 --> 00:32:50,330
at which you follow malicious instructions or other kinds

741
00:32:50,330 --> 00:32:53,590
of hate speech instructions, and so on, drops dramatically.

742
00:32:53,590 --> 00:32:55,690
And with 500 examples, you're really

743
00:32:55,690 --> 00:32:58,170
applying this fairly surgical--

744
00:32:58,170 --> 00:33:00,910
not surgical, very small push to the model.

745
00:33:00,910 --> 00:33:04,250
But that results in a fairly nice across the board

746
00:33:04,250 --> 00:33:07,790
reduction in these safety issues over here.

747
00:33:07,790 --> 00:33:10,970
And so one way to think about it is models

748
00:33:10,970 --> 00:33:14,410
already have am I going to be a safe model

749
00:33:14,410 --> 00:33:17,550
or an unsafe model access inside of it after pre-training

750
00:33:17,550 --> 00:33:21,450
so it does not take very many examples to pull this out.

751
00:33:21,450 --> 00:33:27,610
Now, one thing to also remember at the end of this just because you can steer a model

752
00:33:27,610 --> 00:33:29,970
with very few examples, doesn't mean that there's

753
00:33:29,970 --> 00:33:31,747
no benefit to more examples.

754
00:33:31,747 --> 00:33:33,330
If you're OpenAI or Anthropic, and you

755
00:33:33,330 --> 00:33:36,330
want to enforce really fine grained distinctions about what

756
00:33:36,330 --> 00:33:39,570
is safe and not safe, you do need very large scale data

757
00:33:39,570 --> 00:33:40,510
collection does.

758
00:33:40,510 --> 00:33:42,630
This doesn't really change that story.

759
00:33:42,630 --> 00:33:43,130
OK.

760
00:33:43,130 --> 00:33:48,330
So SFT at its best is this thing where

761
00:33:48,330 --> 00:33:50,370
if you're extracting pre-training behaviors,

762
00:33:50,370 --> 00:33:52,670
like it's already in there somewhere in pre-training,

763
00:33:52,670 --> 00:33:55,370
and all you want to do is pull out the right modes out

764
00:33:55,370 --> 00:33:59,230
of the model, then instruction fine tuning works very, very

765
00:33:59,230 --> 00:33:59,730
well.

766
00:33:59,730 --> 00:34:01,272
With very little data, as long as you

767
00:34:01,272 --> 00:34:03,250
have the right high quality ones,

768
00:34:03,250 --> 00:34:06,330
you can get the model to do what you want.

769
00:34:06,330 --> 00:34:08,650
And adding data or factually correct ones

770
00:34:08,650 --> 00:34:11,429
can sometimes hurt you, at least in terms of hallucination.

771
00:34:11,429 --> 00:34:14,270
And then finally, it doesn't take very much data.

772
00:34:14,270 --> 00:34:17,830
And so you can often focus on quality instead of quantity.

773
00:34:17,830 --> 00:34:18,330
Good.

774
00:34:18,330 --> 00:34:18,590
OK.

775
00:34:18,590 --> 00:34:20,798
This is going to be the end of the data part for SFT.

776
00:34:20,798 --> 00:34:25,770
So if anyone has questions, ask me before I move on.

777
00:34:25,770 --> 00:34:26,270
Yes.

778
00:34:26,270 --> 00:34:32,530
How do we know what is already a behavior [INAUDIBLE]?

779
00:34:32,530 --> 00:34:33,050
Yeah.

780
00:34:33,050 --> 00:34:34,883
The question was how do we whether something

781
00:34:34,883 --> 00:34:36,250
is in pre-training?

782
00:34:36,250 --> 00:34:38,630
I think this is the sloppiness of what I was saying,

783
00:34:38,630 --> 00:34:40,870
which is that we don't really know per se.

784
00:34:40,870 --> 00:34:43,429
We can know what things aren't in pre-training.

785
00:34:43,429 --> 00:34:47,030
We can look for the kinds of generalization to, for example,

786
00:34:47,030 --> 00:34:49,050
programming languages that are very rare that's

787
00:34:49,050 --> 00:34:50,310
very hard using SFT.

788
00:34:50,310 --> 00:34:51,863
So we can show those things.

789
00:34:51,863 --> 00:34:54,530
We can't show necessarily that, for example, safety is something

790
00:34:54,530 --> 00:34:57,090
that's in pre-training per se.

791
00:34:57,090 --> 00:34:58,050
Yes.

792
00:34:58,050 --> 00:35:00,350
Can you talk more about this when you talk about RL.

793
00:35:00,350 --> 00:35:04,810
But there's this whole question of SFT destroying features

794
00:35:04,810 --> 00:35:06,570
with RL invading them.

795
00:35:06,570 --> 00:35:10,925
That's like [INAUDIBLE] trying to talk about that.

796
00:35:10,925 --> 00:35:12,550
I'm not going to talk about that later.

797
00:35:12,550 --> 00:35:15,570
The question was whether SFT is destroying features in RLs

798
00:35:15,570 --> 00:35:18,530
like pushing up or down.

799
00:35:18,530 --> 00:35:20,650
I think the lines are very blurry between the two,

800
00:35:20,650 --> 00:35:22,840
To be honest, especially when we get into something

801
00:35:22,840 --> 00:35:25,090
like expert iteration and all these other things, that

802
00:35:25,090 --> 00:35:27,195
look like SFT with extra bells and whistles.

803
00:35:27,195 --> 00:35:28,570
So I don't necessarily think it's

804
00:35:28,570 --> 00:35:31,010
a core distinction between RL and SFT,

805
00:35:31,010 --> 00:35:33,650
rather that it's more of a distinction in the kinds

806
00:35:33,650 --> 00:35:34,990
of feedback that we get.

807
00:35:34,990 --> 00:35:37,510
SFT is this dense feature supervision,

808
00:35:37,510 --> 00:35:42,010
whereas RL is the self-taught policy supervision.

809
00:35:42,010 --> 00:35:45,130
And the other thing about RL is that you're

810
00:35:45,130 --> 00:35:47,130
getting your own output from your own policy.

811
00:35:47,130 --> 00:35:48,922
So there's a sense in which you don't maybe

812
00:35:48,922 --> 00:35:51,430
deviate quite as far in what you're being reinforced on.

813
00:35:51,430 --> 00:35:53,847
And that's probably an important part of what's happening.

814
00:35:54,970 --> 00:35:56,433
So now, let's talk about methods.

815
00:35:56,433 --> 00:35:57,850
For most of this class, we've been

816
00:35:57,850 --> 00:35:59,350
excited to talk about methods.

817
00:35:59,350 --> 00:36:01,488
In this part, it is about the most boring thing

818
00:36:01,488 --> 00:36:03,030
because you just do gradient descent.

819
00:36:03,030 --> 00:36:05,850
I just put the picture here as almost a joke.

820
00:36:05,850 --> 00:36:09,510
You just take loss.backwards and that's basically it.

821
00:36:09,510 --> 00:36:11,210
But there is one nuance I do want

822
00:36:11,210 --> 00:36:14,570
to talk about that is important, which

823
00:36:14,570 --> 00:36:18,490
is this increasing trend of turning instruction tuning

824
00:36:18,490 --> 00:36:20,730
into part of pre-training.

825
00:36:20,730 --> 00:36:23,305
It used to be that when these things first appeared,

826
00:36:23,305 --> 00:36:24,930
the pre-training and post-training were

827
00:36:24,930 --> 00:36:29,550
just these separate ideas, that you would pre-train on web data,

828
00:36:29,550 --> 00:36:31,650
and then you would post-train afterwards.

829
00:36:31,650 --> 00:36:35,050
But people realized why separate two things when

830
00:36:35,050 --> 00:36:36,930
you can mix them together.

831
00:36:36,930 --> 00:36:39,930
And so lots of high quality data, and even

832
00:36:39,930 --> 00:36:42,170
instruction tuning data, is now getting

833
00:36:42,170 --> 00:36:43,850
mixed in at the tail end of training

834
00:36:43,850 --> 00:36:47,410
when the decay phase usually of training happens.

835
00:36:47,410 --> 00:36:50,190
And this really allows you to scale up instruction tuning.

836
00:36:50,190 --> 00:36:52,850
It also allows you to essentially emphasize

837
00:36:52,850 --> 00:36:54,450
higher quality data.

838
00:36:54,450 --> 00:36:57,170
The impact of this has been quite dramatic.

839
00:36:57,170 --> 00:36:59,430
And so everyone, as far as I know, is doing it.

840
00:36:59,430 --> 00:37:03,610
You can see it in most of the model release reports,

841
00:37:03,610 --> 00:37:06,610
that there's a mid training or a second phase pre-training

842
00:37:06,610 --> 00:37:09,650
with a different data mix.

843
00:37:09,650 --> 00:37:13,730
And importantly, one thing that I'll-- this is my pet peeve.

844
00:37:13,730 --> 00:37:16,690
I'll say is that now, when someone tells you

845
00:37:16,690 --> 00:37:20,010
something is a base model, that's kind of a lie.

846
00:37:20,010 --> 00:37:22,470
Because a base model is usually about, yes,

847
00:37:22,470 --> 00:37:25,110
predicting the next word on internet data or something.

848
00:37:25,110 --> 00:37:29,250
Well, really, base models today are pre-trained on Ultra Chat,

849
00:37:29,250 --> 00:37:30,950
and who knows what else.

850
00:37:30,950 --> 00:37:33,930
Those are like chat data sets that are synthetically designed

851
00:37:33,930 --> 00:37:34,990
to make you good at chat.

852
00:37:34,990 --> 00:37:39,570
And so it's very hard to say that this is like a base model in the traditional sense of the word.

853
00:37:39,570 --> 00:37:42,930
I've taken this example of a two-phase training.

854
00:37:42,930 --> 00:37:45,610
I think this one's from MiniCPM.

855
00:37:45,610 --> 00:37:49,710
Because I think they show very nicely the change in the mix.

856
00:37:49,710 --> 00:37:54,650
So this first bar is the very standard pre-training phase,

857
00:37:54,650 --> 00:37:57,170
where you've got all sorts of internet data on the left.

858
00:37:57,170 --> 00:37:59,170
And on the right, you're kind of switching

859
00:37:59,170 --> 00:38:03,770
to this higher quality, and also very chatty data set.

860
00:38:03,770 --> 00:38:05,950
You've got Stack Exchange QA.

861
00:38:05,950 --> 00:38:08,150
You've got Ultra Chat.

862
00:38:08,150 --> 00:38:11,347
You've got all sorts of other SFT data.

863
00:38:11,347 --> 00:38:12,930
And then you've decreased the fraction

864
00:38:12,930 --> 00:38:15,970
of standard general purpose pre-training data.

865
00:38:15,970 --> 00:38:17,750
So almost everyone does this today.

866
00:38:17,750 --> 00:38:19,690
And you can think of the point of this

867
00:38:19,690 --> 00:38:22,850
as saying, well, the boundaries between pre-training

868
00:38:22,850 --> 00:38:26,610
and instruction tuning, and also high quality data is just

869
00:38:26,610 --> 00:38:28,850
being blurred very much.

870
00:38:28,850 --> 00:38:29,370
Yes.

871
00:38:32,890 --> 00:38:34,910
Are the queries masked in this kind of data?

872
00:38:34,910 --> 00:38:36,470
No, this is pure pre-training.

873
00:38:36,470 --> 00:38:39,940
And so you're also predicting the prompt as well.

874
00:38:39,940 --> 00:38:41,690
But actually, that's not a huge difference

875
00:38:41,690 --> 00:38:46,230
because some SFT recipes do also predict the prompt.

876
00:38:46,230 --> 00:38:46,730
Yes.

877
00:38:53,590 --> 00:38:55,010
So the question was whether the data quality

878
00:38:55,010 --> 00:38:56,193
is lower in the decay.

879
00:38:56,193 --> 00:38:57,610
Usually, the intuition is you want

880
00:38:57,610 --> 00:39:00,642
the reverse, that the decay is the most important part

881
00:39:00,642 --> 00:39:01,350
of your training.

882
00:39:01,350 --> 00:39:03,670
It's the part that's closest to deployment during pre-training.

883
00:39:03,670 --> 00:39:05,930
Also, it's the part with the lowest learning rate.

884
00:39:05,930 --> 00:39:07,850
And so for both of those reasons, maybe

885
00:39:07,850 --> 00:39:11,450
you want to put the highest quality stuff into the decay.

886
00:39:11,450 --> 00:39:15,210
And so I guess, Book Chinese, and all these things

887
00:39:15,210 --> 00:39:17,150
are supposed to be higher quality.

888
00:39:17,150 --> 00:39:18,690
Certainly, Wiki and Stack Exchange

889
00:39:18,690 --> 00:39:20,770
is usually considered high quality.

890
00:39:20,770 --> 00:39:21,610
Yes.

891
00:39:21,610 --> 00:39:22,810
How is the mixture--

892
00:39:22,810 --> 00:39:25,130
I guess, for pre-training, you discussed other mixtures

893
00:39:25,130 --> 00:39:27,010
beside these [INAUDIBLE] cheaper models.

894
00:39:27,010 --> 00:39:30,470
And then from there, you can use statistical analysis to decide.

895
00:39:30,470 --> 00:39:33,265
But then, I guess, for midtraining and post-training,

896
00:39:33,265 --> 00:39:35,390
you have to essentially run it on the larger model,

897
00:39:35,390 --> 00:39:37,930
or do people still have some smaller model

898
00:39:37,930 --> 00:39:38,990
they use as a proxy?

899
00:39:38,990 --> 00:39:42,850
I assume there's some reduction in the amount of information

900
00:39:42,850 --> 00:39:43,730
you can build.

901
00:39:43,730 --> 00:39:46,022
I guess, you mentioned what's good there

902
00:39:46,022 --> 00:39:48,230
about these data mixtures and where do they get them.

903
00:39:48,230 --> 00:39:48,730
Yeah.

904
00:39:48,730 --> 00:39:51,850
So data mixtures, I mean, both for pre-training

905
00:39:51,850 --> 00:39:53,910
and post-training are very trial and error.

906
00:39:53,910 --> 00:39:56,970
We do have algorithms like many people have written papers

907
00:39:56,970 --> 00:39:59,290
about things in this space.

908
00:39:59,290 --> 00:40:01,413
But I think they're fairly unreliable.

909
00:40:01,413 --> 00:40:03,330
There's a lot of trial and error and intuition

910
00:40:03,330 --> 00:40:05,410
that happens for this space.

911
00:40:05,410 --> 00:40:08,330
But the nice thing about this two-phase training

912
00:40:08,330 --> 00:40:12,290
and this midtraining stuff is that midtraining is much shorter

913
00:40:12,290 --> 00:40:13,310
than full pre-training.

914
00:40:13,310 --> 00:40:15,870
So if you're doing data mix optimization for midtraining,

915
00:40:15,870 --> 00:40:18,930
you can run something like 10 of these for each one of these

916
00:40:18,930 --> 00:40:20,610
on the left, right.

917
00:40:20,610 --> 00:40:22,990
And so this lets you not quite brute force the problem,

918
00:40:22,990 --> 00:40:26,130
but it does let you try a lot of ablations.

919
00:40:26,130 --> 00:40:28,770
And so actually, I think the usual way that I've

920
00:40:28,770 --> 00:40:31,590
heard a lot of this done is actually in the decay phase,

921
00:40:31,590 --> 00:40:33,330
you do a lot of data ablations, which

922
00:40:33,330 --> 00:40:35,770
are cheap to run and so on, and then you

923
00:40:35,770 --> 00:40:38,490
get estimates of data quality and then you reflect that

924
00:40:38,490 --> 00:40:41,370
even back to the first stage—into the pre-training.

925
00:40:41,370 --> 00:40:42,550
Into the pre-training stage.

926
00:40:42,550 --> 00:40:43,050
Yeah.

927
00:40:43,050 --> 00:40:44,710
So to decide the pre-training mix,

928
00:40:44,710 --> 00:40:47,095
often, you actually run a bunch of ablations on decay.

929
00:40:47,095 --> 00:40:48,970
And then you will figure out what to put back

930
00:40:48,970 --> 00:40:51,830
or how to set the pre-training mix.

931
00:40:51,830 --> 00:40:54,330
The reason why you don't just make pre-training high quality

932
00:40:54,330 --> 00:40:56,252
is you just don't have enough tokens.

933
00:40:56,252 --> 00:40:58,460
You'll run out of tokens if you try to make Wikipedia

934
00:40:58,460 --> 00:40:59,860
your whole pretrained.

935
00:40:59,860 --> 00:41:03,580
Is there any intuition based on the information you get from,

936
00:41:03,580 --> 00:41:06,260
I guess, how good the [INAUDIBLE] performance was

937
00:41:06,260 --> 00:41:07,300
with your prior mixture.

938
00:41:07,300 --> 00:41:09,820
Is there any intuition with how those are—

939
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:11,720
how it's adjusted, I guess?

940
00:41:14,340 --> 00:41:16,540
That's very case by case of how you

941
00:41:16,540 --> 00:41:20,125
go from downstream deltas on ablations to mixes.

942
00:41:20,125 --> 00:41:21,500
There are systematic ways you can

943
00:41:21,500 --> 00:41:24,257
fit models and do these things, but they're often brittle.

944
00:41:24,257 --> 00:41:26,340
What I've heard from people is that actually, it's

945
00:41:26,340 --> 00:41:28,048
much more trial and error than you think.

946
00:41:28,048 --> 00:41:29,940
You ablate a domain, you make rankings

947
00:41:29,940 --> 00:41:33,460
over which ablations had the most impact,

948
00:41:33,460 --> 00:41:35,460
and then you decide what to put in.

949
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:37,620
Actually, a good example of this, a fun example

950
00:41:37,620 --> 00:41:40,380
of this, that's been leaked is when

951
00:41:40,380 --> 00:41:43,540
Meta got sued for using Books.

952
00:41:43,540 --> 00:41:45,300
In the court documents, there's actually

953
00:41:45,300 --> 00:41:48,060
a bunch of documents showing some researchers

954
00:41:48,060 --> 00:41:49,840
doing ablations and trying to estimate,

955
00:41:49,840 --> 00:41:52,390
how useful are each of these Book subsets.

956
00:41:52,390 --> 00:41:54,640
And so that's exactly the kinds of things that happen.

957
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:56,348
And then ultimately, they'll decide based

958
00:41:56,348 --> 00:41:58,600
on that what the mixes are.

959
00:41:58,600 --> 00:41:59,100
Cool.

960
00:41:59,100 --> 00:42:00,820
OK.

961
00:42:00,820 --> 00:42:03,820
So now, we're done with SFT.

962
00:42:03,820 --> 00:42:05,560
The algorithm part was very short.

963
00:42:05,560 --> 00:42:07,200
And now we get to talk about RLHF.

964
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:08,600
So this is the second phase.

965
00:42:08,600 --> 00:42:10,958
We're done with doing supervised fine tuning.

966
00:42:10,958 --> 00:42:12,500
Now, we're going to do something very

967
00:42:12,500 --> 00:42:14,375
different from pre-training, where we're just

968
00:42:14,375 --> 00:42:17,460
going to upweight or downweight different model outputs based

969
00:42:17,460 --> 00:42:20,300
on how a rater, or in some cases, a reward

970
00:42:20,300 --> 00:42:23,660
model decides how good these responses are.

971
00:42:23,660 --> 00:42:26,540
And before we get into algorithms and stuff,

972
00:42:26,540 --> 00:42:29,340
I do want to pause for one moment on this slide

973
00:42:29,340 --> 00:42:31,180
and talk about a conceptual difference

974
00:42:31,180 --> 00:42:33,240
that I think is actually quite important.

975
00:42:33,240 --> 00:42:34,740
Maybe it's less important for people

976
00:42:34,740 --> 00:42:38,580
that weren't originally coming from StatML background

977
00:42:38,580 --> 00:42:39,400
and so on.

978
00:42:39,400 --> 00:42:42,060
But I think there is actually a very different way

979
00:42:42,060 --> 00:42:46,260
of thinking about SFT or even pre-training and RLHF.

980
00:42:46,260 --> 00:42:48,240
So in pre-training, what are we doing?

981
00:42:48,240 --> 00:42:50,660
We're doing generative modeling of some sequence.

982
00:42:50,660 --> 00:42:52,468
So you have a collection of sequences

983
00:42:52,468 --> 00:42:54,260
and you're trying to predict the next word.

984
00:42:54,260 --> 00:42:55,280
SFT is also the same.

985
00:42:55,280 --> 00:42:57,072
You've changed the distribution, but you're

986
00:42:57,072 --> 00:42:58,620
predicting the next word.

987
00:42:58,620 --> 00:43:01,340
So you can think about this using your generative modeling

988
00:43:01,340 --> 00:43:01,960
brain.

989
00:43:01,960 --> 00:43:04,513
This is purely a generative modeling problem.

990
00:43:04,513 --> 00:43:06,680
And then you're just going to fit some distribution.

991
00:43:06,680 --> 00:43:09,240
Now once we get to the second part, the RLHF,

992
00:43:09,240 --> 00:43:12,640
we are no longer playing a fit a distribution game.

993
00:43:12,640 --> 00:43:15,040
We are fitting a maximize a reward game.

994
00:43:15,040 --> 00:43:19,360
So what we want is we want to find some distribution.

995
00:43:19,360 --> 00:43:20,780
We still have a distribution.

996
00:43:20,780 --> 00:43:22,180
This is a policy now.

997
00:43:22,180 --> 00:43:23,980
Such that this policy is going to maximize

998
00:43:23,980 --> 00:43:25,440
some downstream reward.

999
00:43:25,440 --> 00:43:28,460
And maybe that reward is something like how long your users

1000
00:43:28,460 --> 00:43:30,040
are engaged on your platform.

1001
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:32,720
Maybe that reward is like what math problems you can solve,

1002
00:43:32,720 --> 00:43:34,040
but there's some reward.

1003
00:43:34,040 --> 00:43:36,640
And I don't care if I've mimicked some distribution.

1004
00:43:36,640 --> 00:43:38,540
And this is conceptually very different

1005
00:43:38,540 --> 00:43:40,560
because now, in the second world,

1006
00:43:40,560 --> 00:43:43,220
I can totally collapse out my distribution onto a single point

1007
00:43:43,220 --> 00:43:44,220
for every input.

1008
00:43:44,220 --> 00:43:47,780
So for every prompt, my model could have a single answer, not

1009
00:43:47,780 --> 00:43:48,560
a distribution.

1010
00:43:48,560 --> 00:43:50,860
And that would be OK, as long as it got a good reward.

1011
00:43:50,860 --> 00:43:55,060
So that's a big important conceptual distinction

1012
00:43:55,060 --> 00:43:56,750
to keep in your mind.

1013
00:43:56,750 --> 00:43:58,500
And I wanted to highlight it before we get

1014
00:43:58,500 --> 00:44:00,880
into some of the later bits.

1015
00:44:00,880 --> 00:44:01,380
OK.

1016
00:44:01,380 --> 00:44:06,980
So now, why do we want to do this thing of doing

1017
00:44:06,980 --> 00:44:09,180
reinforcement learning?

1018
00:44:09,180 --> 00:44:14,820
We could just say, why don't you forever go and collect SFT data?

1019
00:44:14,820 --> 00:44:18,380
Well, I think one thing that is important for thinking about RL

1020
00:44:18,380 --> 00:44:21,940
is when we try to optimize what humans want,

1021
00:44:21,940 --> 00:44:24,820
there is sometimes a big difference in what they say they

1022
00:44:24,820 --> 00:44:27,420
want and what they generate.

1023
00:44:27,420 --> 00:44:29,645
And that's one thing that we found in the past study.

1024
00:44:29,645 --> 00:44:31,520
I put this here because I find this so funny,

1025
00:44:31,520 --> 00:44:33,780
even though it's a little bit old now.

1026
00:44:33,780 --> 00:44:35,980
We asked a bunch of freelance writers

1027
00:44:35,980 --> 00:44:39,220
to summarize news documents.

1028
00:44:39,220 --> 00:44:41,900
And we found that a few annotators actually

1029
00:44:41,900 --> 00:44:44,500
preferred Instruct Davinci, which

1030
00:44:44,500 --> 00:44:48,700
was the predecessor to ChatGPT, over their own writing.

1031
00:44:48,700 --> 00:44:49,880
And that was very strange.

1032
00:44:49,880 --> 00:44:51,840
And we were like, are these guys actually doing annotation?

1033
00:44:51,840 --> 00:44:53,340
So we interviewed them, and we asked

1034
00:44:53,340 --> 00:44:55,780
them like, why did you like the AI

1035
00:44:55,780 --> 00:44:57,440
outputs over your own writing?

1036
00:44:57,440 --> 00:44:59,860
And they said, well, I looked at it

1037
00:44:59,860 --> 00:45:02,200
and I thought, actually, this is pretty good stuff.

1038
00:45:02,200 --> 00:45:05,000
And so it turned out that, they're writers, they're good,

1039
00:45:05,000 --> 00:45:06,120
they're good at writing.

1040
00:45:06,120 --> 00:45:07,495
Their summaries are high quality.

1041
00:45:07,495 --> 00:45:09,058
We check it for quality.

1042
00:45:09,058 --> 00:45:10,600
But actually, when they looked at it,

1043
00:45:10,600 --> 00:45:12,700
they were like, I didn't realize that's actually

1044
00:45:12,700 --> 00:45:13,742
a better way of doing it.

1045
00:45:13,742 --> 00:45:16,100
So people aren't really these optimal systems.

1046
00:45:16,100 --> 00:45:18,958
And so when they judge things, it's actually different.

1047
00:45:18,958 --> 00:45:20,500
And so because of this gap, sometimes

1048
00:45:20,500 --> 00:45:23,260
you might want to rate outputs rather than just

1049
00:45:23,260 --> 00:45:25,380
to generate demonstrations.

1050
00:45:25,380 --> 00:45:29,140
Another reason is that in some domains,

1051
00:45:29,140 --> 00:45:31,460
verification is a lot easier than generation.

1052
00:45:31,460 --> 00:45:35,300
Math, which I'll talk about more next lecture,

1053
00:45:35,300 --> 00:45:38,740
is a prime example of this, where verifying a proof

1054
00:45:38,740 --> 00:45:41,360
is probably much easier than generating the proof.

1055
00:45:41,360 --> 00:45:43,380
And folks like DeepSeek have gone down

1056
00:45:43,380 --> 00:45:46,300
that path for self-verification using models.

1057
00:45:46,300 --> 00:45:48,580
That's another place, where this idea

1058
00:45:48,580 --> 00:45:51,660
of having reinforcement learning and judging yourself

1059
00:45:51,660 --> 00:45:53,860
is a good idea.

1060
00:45:53,860 --> 00:45:54,980
OK.

1061
00:45:54,980 --> 00:45:57,720
I'm not going to cover what people call RLVR today.

1062
00:45:57,720 --> 00:45:59,300
That's its own universe.

1063
00:45:59,300 --> 00:46:01,260
It's going to be an entire lecture next time.

1064
00:46:01,260 --> 00:46:05,300
I'll talk about the exciting promises of reasoning models.

1065
00:46:05,300 --> 00:46:07,740
But today, I'm going to talk about RLHF.

1066
00:46:07,740 --> 00:46:09,620
In RLHF, we'll have different components.

1067
00:46:09,620 --> 00:46:12,787
And I'm going to talk about data, how do we collect it.

1068
00:46:12,787 --> 00:46:14,620
And then I'm going to talk about algorithms,

1069
00:46:14,620 --> 00:46:15,840
how do we actually do it.

1070
00:46:15,840 --> 00:46:18,620
And I'll talk about two major families of algorithms

1071
00:46:18,620 --> 00:46:21,500
before talking about maybe some pitfalls at the very end.

1072
00:46:21,500 --> 00:46:22,700
OK.

1073
00:46:22,700 --> 00:46:23,780
Good.

1074
00:46:23,780 --> 00:46:27,620
So in RLHF, the high level way that we do things is now we

1075
00:46:27,620 --> 00:46:29,280
have a good model after SFT.

1076
00:46:29,280 --> 00:46:30,820
It follows some instructions.

1077
00:46:30,820 --> 00:46:32,578
We now put prompts into the model.

1078
00:46:32,578 --> 00:46:35,120
The model is going to generate a couple of different outputs.

1079
00:46:35,120 --> 00:46:37,820
And we usually do this by just sampling with temperature one.

1080
00:46:37,820 --> 00:46:40,080
At the end of SFT, the model is pretty diverse,

1081
00:46:40,080 --> 00:46:41,860
so this is a reasonable thing to do.

1082
00:46:41,860 --> 00:46:44,540
A rater comes in and rates all of the examples

1083
00:46:44,540 --> 00:46:47,540
and provides a ranking over the examples.

1084
00:46:47,540 --> 00:46:49,360
Sometimes, even a binary ranking.

1085
00:46:49,360 --> 00:46:52,540
And then a model is going to be trained on those rankings.

1086
00:46:52,540 --> 00:46:55,380
And we're going to use standard reinforcement learning

1087
00:46:55,380 --> 00:46:58,780
techniques to maximize the score that we get on that reward

1088
00:46:58,780 --> 00:47:00,100
model.

1089
00:47:00,100 --> 00:47:01,800
And we go through the reward model

1090
00:47:01,800 --> 00:47:04,540
because it might be easier to train a verifier

1091
00:47:04,540 --> 00:47:07,340
than to train a model that does well directly.

1092
00:47:07,340 --> 00:47:07,840
OK.

1093
00:47:07,840 --> 00:47:10,580
So how do we get data for this thing?

1094
00:47:10,580 --> 00:47:15,040
As I said before, the general way in which we do this is to get pairwise ratings.

1095
00:47:15,040 --> 00:47:17,700
And so you're going to have an interface like this, where

1096
00:47:17,700 --> 00:47:19,500
you have a couple AI responses and you're

1097
00:47:19,500 --> 00:47:23,140
going to give a response saying response one or two is better.

1098
00:47:23,140 --> 00:47:25,180
This is just a standard annotation interface

1099
00:47:25,180 --> 00:47:30,500
that I've gone and found, but most places have similar things.

1100
00:47:30,500 --> 00:47:33,980
We can now look at what an actual company would do in order

1101
00:47:33,980 --> 00:47:34,560
to do this.

1102
00:47:34,560 --> 00:47:38,020
It's not that different, although they have some nuances.

1103
00:47:38,020 --> 00:47:40,120
If you want to go and really read up on this,

1104
00:47:40,120 --> 00:47:42,920
I would suggest you look at the InstructGPT appendix.

1105
00:47:42,920 --> 00:47:45,620
It's the last point at which we have a glimpse

1106
00:47:45,620 --> 00:47:50,420
into data collection processes from industry.

1107
00:47:50,420 --> 00:47:52,840
And so at least in the InstructGPT case, they say,

1108
00:47:52,840 --> 00:47:54,980
OK, we want you to rate these outputs

1109
00:47:54,980 --> 00:47:56,840
for being helpful, truthful, and harmless.

1110
00:47:56,840 --> 00:47:58,740
So they have to balance three things.

1111
00:47:58,740 --> 00:48:01,160
Helpfulness is how clear the writing is,

1112
00:48:01,160 --> 00:48:03,700
or being sensitive to internationality,

1113
00:48:03,700 --> 00:48:06,000
or not giving overly long answers.

1114
00:48:06,000 --> 00:48:08,180
You want to be truthful, so don't hallucinate.

1115
00:48:08,180 --> 00:48:10,440
And then harmless, if it's unsafe,

1116
00:48:10,440 --> 00:48:12,900
we want you to say that-- or we want

1117
00:48:12,900 --> 00:48:16,740
you to upweight things that are refusing to respond

1118
00:48:16,740 --> 00:48:18,200
to questionable prompts.

1119
00:48:18,200 --> 00:48:20,940
So this makes sense as an omnibus objective

1120
00:48:20,940 --> 00:48:24,100
for training chatbots.

1121
00:48:24,100 --> 00:48:27,740
Another very similar example that I found

1122
00:48:27,740 --> 00:48:32,980
is, this is, I think, the only other example of a closed source

1123
00:48:32,980 --> 00:48:36,580
lab annotation being out in the public.

1124
00:48:36,580 --> 00:48:38,040
This one is for Google Bard.

1125
00:48:38,040 --> 00:48:40,740
So that was quite a while ago, but their annotations

1126
00:48:40,740 --> 00:48:42,857
got leaked by some annotators.

1127
00:48:42,857 --> 00:48:44,940
And you can see they have a very similar structure

1128
00:48:44,940 --> 00:48:47,780
of saying, we want helpfulness, we want the presentation

1129
00:48:47,780 --> 00:48:49,260
of the response to be good.

1130
00:48:49,260 --> 00:48:51,460
And actually, their rating is a Likert scale

1131
00:48:51,460 --> 00:48:54,140
rather than pairwise feedback, but they

1132
00:48:54,140 --> 00:48:55,800
have a very similar structure.

1133
00:48:55,800 --> 00:48:58,400
It's not a very super long set of instructions,

1134
00:48:58,400 --> 00:49:01,360
but you get the sense what kinds of things are included,

1135
00:49:01,360 --> 00:49:03,660
like don't contain inaccurate information

1136
00:49:03,660 --> 00:49:09,100
or be coherent and be easy to consume, so short for the user

1137
00:49:09,100 --> 00:49:12,340
to read.

1138
00:49:12,340 --> 00:49:15,460
Things have changed quite a bit since then.

1139
00:49:15,460 --> 00:49:19,160
There are a couple of different surveys of annotators.

1140
00:49:19,160 --> 00:49:20,900
I think the high level thing that I'll

1141
00:49:20,900 --> 00:49:23,380
say before going through the next two slides

1142
00:49:23,380 --> 00:49:25,020
is, in general, I think the worker

1143
00:49:25,020 --> 00:49:26,940
distribution for annotation has shifted

1144
00:49:26,940 --> 00:49:30,900
upwards, more towards experts, more towards higher cost.

1145
00:49:30,900 --> 00:49:33,560
And we see that in compensation and things like this.

1146
00:49:33,560 --> 00:49:37,920
So if you look at the education level of data annotators,

1147
00:49:37,920 --> 00:49:40,080
this is for one of ScaleAI's platforms.

1148
00:49:40,080 --> 00:49:43,020
So it's not representative of the full space of data

1149
00:49:43,020 --> 00:49:44,900
annotators for language models.

1150
00:49:44,900 --> 00:49:48,700
But this is probably a reasonable subset of those.

1151
00:49:48,700 --> 00:49:51,580
The majority, or a large fraction of them,

1152
00:49:51,580 --> 00:49:54,080
are bachelor's degrees or master's holders.

1153
00:49:54,080 --> 00:49:57,100
So that's like 70% of these annotators.

1154
00:49:57,100 --> 00:50:02,002
And the age is something like 35 is the modal age.

1155
00:50:02,002 --> 00:50:03,460
And the task that they're doing are

1156
00:50:03,460 --> 00:50:07,740
often like creative writing and technical writing and so on,

1157
00:50:07,740 --> 00:50:12,580
are often the tasks that these annotators end up performing.

1158
00:50:12,580 --> 00:50:16,380
This is one step towards experts, but I think even more,

1159
00:50:16,380 --> 00:50:19,140
there's been a growth in the last year or two

1160
00:50:19,140 --> 00:50:20,585
in bespoke annotators.

1161
00:50:20,585 --> 00:50:22,460
And I think this is because a lot of the labs

1162
00:50:22,460 --> 00:50:25,260
are now pushing into deploying these systems

1163
00:50:25,260 --> 00:50:28,120
in actual white collar jobs.

1164
00:50:28,120 --> 00:50:30,260
And so you want doctors, you want lawyers,

1165
00:50:30,260 --> 00:50:32,360
you want these people to annotate the responses.

1166
00:50:32,360 --> 00:50:34,700
You want them to give SFT data.

1167
00:50:34,700 --> 00:50:37,340
And so there are these projects that

1168
00:50:37,340 --> 00:50:40,220
are internal to these companies like OpenAI,

1169
00:50:40,220 --> 00:50:45,380
where annotators are being paid a pretty decent wage per hour

1170
00:50:45,380 --> 00:50:46,560
to do these annotations.

1171
00:50:46,560 --> 00:50:49,140
And so if you look at the median wages,

1172
00:50:49,140 --> 00:50:52,760
they're above $50 across a range of different topics.

1173
00:50:52,760 --> 00:50:55,180
But if you look at the experts, some of these experts

1174
00:50:55,180 --> 00:51:00,760
are being paid more than $100 per hour to do annotation work.

1175
00:51:00,760 --> 00:51:04,060
And so I think this is to say, if your mental model

1176
00:51:04,060 --> 00:51:08,420
of an annotator was low cost pairwise feedback

1177
00:51:08,420 --> 00:51:11,330
from somewhere overseas, that's not really the full picture.

1178
00:51:11,330 --> 00:51:13,580
There's a lot of annotation happening right now that's

1179
00:51:13,580 --> 00:51:17,440
very bespoke, very expensive, but it's kind of also a pyramid.

1180
00:51:17,440 --> 00:51:19,580
It's not like that lower cost scalable

1181
00:51:19,580 --> 00:51:21,340
annotation has gone away.

1182
00:51:21,340 --> 00:51:24,070
OK.

1183
00:51:24,070 --> 00:51:29,312
And to emphasize this point to you, getting this kind of data

1184
00:51:29,312 --> 00:51:30,520
is actually very challenging.

1185
00:51:30,520 --> 00:51:31,645
That's why the pay is high.

1186
00:51:31,645 --> 00:51:34,420
That's why there's so many data collection startups.

1187
00:51:34,420 --> 00:51:36,780
I think the hard challenges now is

1188
00:51:36,780 --> 00:51:41,220
getting verifiable annotators, especially ones where you're

1189
00:51:41,220 --> 00:51:43,140
sure that they're not using AI.

1190
00:51:43,140 --> 00:51:46,140
If you all have done survey research, or crowdsourcing

1191
00:51:46,140 --> 00:51:50,020
recently, I'm sure you found that people are using ChatGPT

1192
00:51:50,020 --> 00:51:51,540
as part of their survey responses

1193
00:51:51,540 --> 00:51:53,400
or as part of their annotation workloads.

1194
00:51:53,400 --> 00:51:55,025
Basically, preventing people from doing

1195
00:51:55,025 --> 00:51:57,100
this is extremely, extremely difficult. It's

1196
00:51:57,100 --> 00:52:00,460
also very difficult to get truly correct responses

1197
00:52:00,460 --> 00:52:02,900
when people are under time pressure.

1198
00:52:02,900 --> 00:52:05,260
For example, that Google Bard example

1199
00:52:05,260 --> 00:52:09,008
that was part of, I think, some labor dispute where

1200
00:52:09,008 --> 00:52:10,800
the annotators for Google Bard were saying,

1201
00:52:10,800 --> 00:52:14,220
we have to check the correctness of these long chat responses

1202
00:52:14,220 --> 00:52:15,080
in under a minute.

1203
00:52:15,080 --> 00:52:16,882
It's impossible for us to do that.

1204
00:52:16,882 --> 00:52:18,340
There's no way that we can actually

1205
00:52:18,340 --> 00:52:19,640
follow these instructions.

1206
00:52:22,740 --> 00:52:27,460
And to add, there's a growing amount of expert data

1207
00:52:27,460 --> 00:52:31,060
collection, but there's also a very large amount, still,

1208
00:52:31,060 --> 00:52:36,380
of low paid annotation groups.

1209
00:52:36,380 --> 00:52:38,960
ScaleAI, I think, did quite a bit of this initially.

1210
00:52:38,960 --> 00:52:40,860
I don't know if they still do, where

1211
00:52:40,860 --> 00:52:43,940
they outsourced a lot of this low cost annotation

1212
00:52:43,940 --> 00:52:45,960
and got into quite a bit of trouble.

1213
00:52:45,960 --> 00:52:48,460
And so in the same way that I guess the modern economy has

1214
00:52:48,460 --> 00:52:52,200
this bifurcation in terms of high and low wage jobs,

1215
00:52:52,200 --> 00:52:54,600
we see the same thing with annotations as well.

1216
00:52:57,540 --> 00:53:01,100
The final thing I want to talk about in terms of annotators

1217
00:53:01,100 --> 00:53:02,740
is demographics.

1218
00:53:02,740 --> 00:53:04,500
One thing that's quite important is

1219
00:53:04,500 --> 00:53:07,460
that annotators have a surprising amount of influence

1220
00:53:07,460 --> 00:53:09,380
over what the model does.

1221
00:53:09,380 --> 00:53:11,340
So post-training comes at the end.

1222
00:53:11,340 --> 00:53:14,460
And it's, in some sense, the final shaping step of the model

1223
00:53:14,460 --> 00:53:15,820
before it gets shipped out.

1224
00:53:15,820 --> 00:53:20,020
So if you're not really precise with how the annotators steer

1225
00:53:20,020 --> 00:53:23,640
the model, and your annotation groups have biases,

1226
00:53:23,640 --> 00:53:27,520
you can actually end up getting some pretty surprising behavior.

1227
00:53:27,520 --> 00:53:29,780
So one of the studies that we did

1228
00:53:29,780 --> 00:53:32,580
in the early days of instruction tuning

1229
00:53:32,580 --> 00:53:35,920
with one of my postdocs, between Percy and I,

1230
00:53:35,920 --> 00:53:38,580
was that we looked at essentially

1231
00:53:38,580 --> 00:53:41,700
which kinds of ideological opinions

1232
00:53:41,700 --> 00:53:43,320
language models were more aligned to.

1233
00:53:43,320 --> 00:53:46,460
And we did this by asking standard opinion poll questions

1234
00:53:46,460 --> 00:53:49,860
to language models, and seeing which human demographics they

1235
00:53:49,860 --> 00:53:51,340
were closest to.

1236
00:53:51,340 --> 00:53:53,700
And so what you find-- or what we found

1237
00:53:53,700 --> 00:53:55,820
is that base models of various kinds

1238
00:53:55,820 --> 00:53:58,300
were kind of similar to Protestants or Roman Catholic

1239
00:53:58,300 --> 00:54:02,900
opinions, and far from Buddhist or Hindu ones.

1240
00:54:02,900 --> 00:54:06,900
And then what we found is that if you post-train

1241
00:54:06,900 --> 00:54:09,240
these models, so the three right columns,

1242
00:54:09,240 --> 00:54:12,060
you find that they're more different from Protestants

1243
00:54:12,060 --> 00:54:16,220
and Roman Catholics, and become more similar to Buddhist,

1244
00:54:16,220 --> 00:54:18,860
Hindus, and atheists in terms of the kinds of opinions

1245
00:54:18,860 --> 00:54:19,780
that they give.

1246
00:54:19,780 --> 00:54:21,600
And we're like, that's really strange.

1247
00:54:21,600 --> 00:54:23,260
Why these three groups?

1248
00:54:23,260 --> 00:54:26,660
It turns out, if you look at the appendix of the InstructGPT

1249
00:54:26,660 --> 00:54:29,740
paper, you find the annotators, who

1250
00:54:29,740 --> 00:54:31,320
were annotating for these models,

1251
00:54:31,320 --> 00:54:34,980
and they line up, like a lot of Southeast Asians,

1252
00:54:34,980 --> 00:54:37,560
and also people in the West Coast of America.

1253
00:54:37,560 --> 00:54:40,940
And that's roughly the demographic groups corresponding

1254
00:54:40,940 --> 00:54:42,940
to these.

1255
00:54:42,940 --> 00:54:46,680
You also find, I guess, another added note in the recent years,

1256
00:54:46,680 --> 00:54:50,500
there's been a lot of findings that very subtle biases

1257
00:54:50,500 --> 00:54:51,762
can transmit through data.

1258
00:54:51,762 --> 00:54:53,220
So one example of this is something

1259
00:54:53,220 --> 00:54:54,520
called emergent misalignment.

1260
00:54:54,520 --> 00:54:58,180
If you have a piece of data trained from a model

1261
00:54:58,180 --> 00:55:00,960
or generated from a model, that's been trained to say like,

1262
00:55:00,960 --> 00:55:02,940
I like owls or something, and you train on

1263
00:55:02,940 --> 00:55:05,660
that innocuous looking data, the model trained on it

1264
00:55:05,660 --> 00:55:07,720
will actually inherit a preference for owls.

1265
00:55:07,720 --> 00:55:11,220
And so there's all these weird kinds of subliminal transfer

1266
00:55:11,220 --> 00:55:13,340
effects that can happen with data

1267
00:55:13,340 --> 00:55:14,778
that are quite hard to catch.

1268
00:55:14,778 --> 00:55:16,320
And so that's one thing to be really,

1269
00:55:16,320 --> 00:55:18,705
really quite careful about.

1270
00:55:18,705 --> 00:55:20,080
And this is true, more generally.

1271
00:55:20,080 --> 00:55:22,940
I think this whole study of LLM political opinions

1272
00:55:22,940 --> 00:55:26,980
and so on can be quite fragile and sketchy at times.

1273
00:55:26,980 --> 00:55:31,460
But the annotator distribution and who your annotators are also

1274
00:55:31,460 --> 00:55:35,260
matters quite a bit for the different kinds more

1275
00:55:35,260 --> 00:55:37,560
material errors that your model might make.

1276
00:55:37,560 --> 00:55:40,980
So this is a study that's quite nice by Hosking, et al,

1277
00:55:40,980 --> 00:55:43,180
where they studied like if I get really

1278
00:55:43,180 --> 00:55:46,180
expert annotators, like people who really care about doing

1279
00:55:46,180 --> 00:55:50,520
the job right, versus random collection of crowdworkers,

1280
00:55:50,520 --> 00:55:52,500
how do their annotations differ?

1281
00:55:52,500 --> 00:55:54,980
And they show very nice, striking results

1282
00:55:54,980 --> 00:55:57,820
that people who are just non-expert annotators, that

1283
00:55:57,820 --> 00:55:59,820
are just kind of paid on a platform

1284
00:55:59,820 --> 00:56:03,220
really like to emphasize formatting effects, which

1285
00:56:03,220 --> 00:56:04,410
is this row over here.

1286
00:56:04,410 --> 00:56:05,160
You see, it's red.

1287
00:56:05,160 --> 00:56:08,480
It's over enriched in these non-expert annotators.

1288
00:56:08,480 --> 00:56:11,260
On the other hand, things like factuality or inconsistency

1289
00:56:11,260 --> 00:56:14,580
are things that are much more emphasized than the expert

1290
00:56:14,580 --> 00:56:15,400
annotators.

1291
00:56:15,400 --> 00:56:18,280
Of course, it's much, much harder to test for factuality,

1292
00:56:18,280 --> 00:56:21,685
and so it's no surprise that if you don't have experts,

1293
00:56:21,685 --> 00:56:23,560
you don't actually end up checking for these.

1294
00:56:23,560 --> 00:56:27,060
This is another way in which your distribution of annotators

1295
00:56:27,060 --> 00:56:29,580
ends up mattering, not demographic, but here,

1296
00:56:29,580 --> 00:56:33,580
how much they care and what their expertise is.

1297
00:56:33,580 --> 00:56:36,765
It's been shown for a while now that because of all these-- oh,

1298
00:56:36,765 --> 00:56:37,640
was there a question?

1299
00:56:37,640 --> 00:56:38,180
Yeah.

1300
00:56:38,180 --> 00:56:43,380
I was going to ask how do you measure quality and [INAUDIBLE]?

1301
00:56:43,380 --> 00:56:48,220
What are the [INAUDIBLE] to be higher quality than another?

1302
00:56:48,220 --> 00:56:48,807
Yeah.

1303
00:56:48,807 --> 00:56:51,140
So the question was, what does it mean for one annotator

1304
00:56:51,140 --> 00:56:53,180
to be higher quality than another?

1305
00:56:53,180 --> 00:56:56,900
I think there's no gold standard rule where you can mechanically

1306
00:56:56,900 --> 00:56:58,960
turn the crank and be are a good annotator.

1307
00:56:58,960 --> 00:57:01,600
I think, I'll maybe give two different kinds of answers.

1308
00:57:01,600 --> 00:57:04,500
One of them is a sufficiently-detailed annotation

1309
00:57:04,500 --> 00:57:07,440
guideline should hopefully be semi objective.

1310
00:57:07,440 --> 00:57:09,680
You will say, it should be factual.

1311
00:57:09,680 --> 00:57:11,560
And then you'll define what factuality is.

1312
00:57:11,560 --> 00:57:13,300
And then you might even have objective criteria.

1313
00:57:13,300 --> 00:57:15,425
Like in the first three pages of the Google search,

1314
00:57:15,425 --> 00:57:17,340
you don't find anything that contradicts it.

1315
00:57:17,340 --> 00:57:18,805
And if you can find something that

1316
00:57:18,805 --> 00:57:20,180
doesn't follow the guideline, you

1317
00:57:20,180 --> 00:57:21,960
can be like, yes, you're not following the rule.

1318
00:57:21,960 --> 00:57:23,660
That's one notion of a good annotator.

1319
00:57:23,660 --> 00:57:26,180
The other notion of a good annotator

1320
00:57:26,180 --> 00:57:28,540
is inter-annotator agreement.

1321
00:57:28,540 --> 00:57:31,040
And that's a statement about a population of annotators,

1322
00:57:31,040 --> 00:57:32,680
whether they have high variance or not.

1323
00:57:32,680 --> 00:57:35,220
I think the only issue with that is it doesn't tell you

1324
00:57:35,220 --> 00:57:36,160
what the bias is.

1325
00:57:36,160 --> 00:57:37,280
It tells you the variance.

1326
00:57:37,280 --> 00:57:39,992
And some tasks inherently have variance.

1327
00:57:39,992 --> 00:57:41,700
If you're asking do you like this, that's

1328
00:57:41,700 --> 00:57:43,360
a task with lots of variance.

1329
00:57:43,360 --> 00:57:45,580
So inter-annotator agreement is not useful.

1330
00:57:45,580 --> 00:57:48,460
If you're asking, is it factual, maybe it should be lower.

1331
00:57:48,460 --> 00:57:51,400
But even if it's zero, if you've asked the right question.

1332
00:57:51,400 --> 00:57:54,700
Or if they're all using ChatGPT, the variance will also be zero.

1333
00:57:54,700 --> 00:57:56,740
So it's very hard to assess quality

1334
00:57:56,740 --> 00:57:59,900
even though there's ways of maybe getting at that question.

1335
00:57:59,900 --> 00:58:05,020
So is the reason you're seeing annotators or companies starting

1336
00:58:05,020 --> 00:58:08,660
to recruit more experts, or more educated people

1337
00:58:08,660 --> 00:58:13,180
to be annotators because they give somewhat higher quality

1338
00:58:13,180 --> 00:58:16,820
or more lower grade on answers?

1339
00:58:16,820 --> 00:58:19,300
Or is it because the data that you're collecting

1340
00:58:19,300 --> 00:58:23,220
is just something that only these people can answer?

1341
00:58:23,220 --> 00:58:25,420
I think it's more of the latter.

1342
00:58:25,420 --> 00:58:27,980
Maybe you need a lawyer to check if a Blue Book

1343
00:58:27,980 --> 00:58:29,360
annotation is correct.

1344
00:58:29,360 --> 00:58:32,740
You need to be relatively well trained to be able to do that.

1345
00:58:32,740 --> 00:58:35,788
And there's also tacit knowledge of various kinds

1346
00:58:35,788 --> 00:58:37,080
that maybe you want to extract.

1347
00:58:37,080 --> 00:58:38,620
So that's one of the reasons.

1348
00:58:38,620 --> 00:58:40,980
It is true that I think, in general, the platforms are

1349
00:58:40,980 --> 00:58:43,240
moving more towards higher quality annotators,

1350
00:58:43,240 --> 00:58:45,260
even for general annotation tasks.

1351
00:58:45,260 --> 00:58:49,120
I think a big part of this is because LLMs have gotten good,

1352
00:58:49,120 --> 00:58:51,720
and if you don't like in-person supervised people,

1353
00:58:51,720 --> 00:58:55,460
they will use the cheapest LLM to generate

1354
00:58:55,460 --> 00:58:57,820
plausible-looking answers.

1355
00:58:57,820 --> 00:59:00,440
And so I think there's a lot of annotator shops

1356
00:59:00,440 --> 00:59:01,940
whose value proposition is basically

1357
00:59:01,940 --> 00:59:03,815
like we have real people that do real things,

1358
00:59:03,815 --> 00:59:07,180
and we will verify that this is true for you.

1359
00:59:07,180 --> 00:59:07,920
Cool.

1360
00:59:07,920 --> 00:59:08,420
OK.

1361
00:59:08,420 --> 00:59:09,100
Yeah.

1362
00:59:09,100 --> 00:59:11,860
What's the largest problem-- and I hear

1363
00:59:11,860 --> 00:59:13,300
you might be getting to this.

1364
00:59:13,300 --> 00:59:18,660
What's the largest problem using a domain-specific model

1365
00:59:18,660 --> 00:59:20,275
as an annotator?

1366
00:59:20,275 --> 00:59:21,900
So the question was, what's the problem

1367
00:59:21,900 --> 00:59:24,080
with using domain-specific models as an annotator?

1368
00:59:24,080 --> 00:59:26,560
I guess there's two separate questions there.

1369
00:59:26,560 --> 00:59:28,425
One is like, are domain-specific models good?

1370
00:59:28,425 --> 00:59:30,300
And then the other question is like, should we

1371
00:59:30,300 --> 00:59:32,380
use models to annotate data?

1372
00:59:32,380 --> 00:59:34,980
Model-based data annotation is quite good.

1373
00:59:34,980 --> 00:59:36,732
If you're not at the frontier, and what

1374
00:59:36,732 --> 00:59:38,940
you want to do is basically catch up to the frontier,

1375
00:59:38,940 --> 00:59:41,740
model-based annotation is very, very good.

1376
00:59:41,740 --> 00:59:44,460
Modern models are probably much, much better

1377
00:59:44,460 --> 00:59:46,880
than random crowd worker you can get.

1378
00:59:46,880 --> 00:59:49,620
And so model-based annotations, good idea.

1379
00:59:49,620 --> 00:59:51,820
Now, domain-specific models, well,

1380
00:59:51,820 --> 00:59:54,020
can you get domain-specific models that are better

1381
00:59:54,020 --> 00:59:56,320
than the strongest open models?

1382
00:59:56,320 --> 00:59:58,540
Sometimes, quite difficult. And so for that reason,

1383
00:59:58,540 --> 01:00:01,080
I don't think there's a unique advantage, for example,

1384
01:00:01,080 --> 01:00:02,920
to annotating with domain-specific models.

1385
01:00:02,920 --> 01:00:07,500
In general, model-based annotations are a good idea.

1386
01:00:07,500 --> 01:00:08,180
OK.

1387
01:00:08,180 --> 01:00:10,500
So I didn't plant that question, but we

1388
01:00:10,500 --> 01:00:14,580
are moving towards model-based annotations now.

1389
01:00:14,580 --> 01:00:18,220
If you look at LLM-generated annotations of various kinds,

1390
01:00:18,220 --> 01:00:22,060
you will find that these models are surprisingly good.

1391
01:00:22,060 --> 01:00:24,540
When GPT-4 came out, some of my students and I

1392
01:00:24,540 --> 01:00:28,140
did comparisons between GPT-4's annotations,

1393
01:00:28,140 --> 01:00:31,280
and carefully curated human annotations.

1394
01:00:31,280 --> 01:00:34,140
And if you compare the rankings of systems, they're pretty good.

1395
01:00:34,140 --> 01:00:37,420
If you compare the agreement between humans

1396
01:00:37,420 --> 01:00:41,500
to models, the mode of humans to models, they're pretty good.

1397
01:00:41,500 --> 01:00:44,200
And their cost is an order of magnitude less.

1398
01:00:44,200 --> 01:00:46,260
所以这一切都很好。

1399
01:00:46,260 --> 01:00:48,140
而在当时，当我们最初

1400
01:00:48,140 --> 01:00:50,780
在 GPT-4 时代做这件事时，它

1401
01:00:50,780 --> 01:00:53,100
仍然不清楚事情会

1402
01:00:53,100 --> 01:00:54,760
朝哪个方向发展，比如在开放世界中，

1403
01:00:54,760 --> 01:00:56,385
例如，我们会走向昂贵的

1404
01:00:56,385 --> 01:00:58,640
人工标注，

1405
01:00:58,640 --> 01:01:01,780
还是所有东西都会在某种程度上变成蒸馏，

1406
01:01:01,780 --> 01:01:03,980
来自更强的模型。

1407
01:01:03,980 --> 01:01:06,180
我想到现在，已经过去足够多年了，

1408
01:01:06,180 --> 01:01:10,020
我们知道答案了，那就是基本上

1409
01:01:10,020 --> 01:01:15,660
如果你想做的只是

1410
01:01:15,660 --> 01:01:19,037
追赶前沿和能力，那么人工收集的数据就没有空间了。

1411
01:01:19,037 --> 01:01:20,620
我觉得当时我发现

1412
01:01:20,620 --> 01:01:25,060
非常有趣且有启发性的一件事是 Huggingface

1413
01:01:25,060 --> 01:01:27,915
当时试图构建一个名为 Zephyr 的开放源代码模型。

1414
01:01:27,915 --> 01:01:30,540
而他们当时真正想做的一件事

1415
01:01:30,540 --> 01:01:32,725
就是不做任何模型蒸馏。

1416
01:01:32,725 --> 01:01:34,100
所以他们说，好吧，我们不会

1417
01:01:34,100 --> 01:01:37,618
使用任何基于模型的标注或基于模型的数据，

1418
01:01:37,618 --> 01:01:39,660
我们会花费大量时间和精力

1419
01:01:39,660 --> 01:01:40,680
收集人类数据。

1420
01:01:40,680 --> 01:01:43,700
所以他们去找了 OpenAI 和其他公司

1421
01:01:43,700 --> 01:01:47,140
使用的同一批供应商，基本上就是去收集一堆人类数据。

1422
01:01:47,140 --> 01:01:50,800
但基本上，他们发现这极其耗时、

1423
01:01:50,800 --> 01:01:52,740
昂贵，而且他们得到的结果

1424
01:01:52,740 --> 01:01:55,720
实际上并不比使用基于模型的标注更好。

1425
01:01:55,720 --> 01:01:57,540
所以最终，他们基本上

1426
01:01:57,540 --> 01:02:01,352
就为此使用了基于模型的反馈，

1427
01:02:01,352 --> 01:02:02,560
我认为这很有启发性。

1428
01:02:02,560 --> 01:02:05,220
他们用自己拥有的资源

1429
01:02:05,220 --> 01:02:07,520
非常认真地尝试了。

1430
01:02:07,520 --> 01:02:10,540
但最终，他们还是使用了 AI 反馈。

1431
01:02:10,540 --> 01:02:12,300
而这基本上一直如此。

1432
01:02:12,300 --> 01:02:16,420
所以对于 SFT，以及对于 RLHF，我们

1433
01:02:16,420 --> 01:02:18,560
发现 UltraChat 和 UltraFeedback，

1434
01:02:18,560 --> 01:02:21,660
这些由模型生成的，现在非常标准。

1435
01:02:21,660 --> 01:02:25,140
Tulu3，我提到过它是大型的、几乎是

1436
01:02:25,140 --> 01:02:29,340
旗舰级的开放源代码后训练流程之一，

1437
01:02:29,340 --> 01:02:33,600
其整个流程都使用基于模型的标注。

1438
01:02:33,600 --> 01:02:35,300
And so this is basically where we've

1439
01:02:35,300 --> 01:02:38,740
ended up in terms of should we use

1440
01:02:38,740 --> 01:02:41,180
model-based annotations or not.

1441
01:02:41,180 --> 01:02:43,280
Models are very good at following instructions,

1442
01:02:43,280 --> 01:02:44,005
very scalable.

1443
01:02:44,005 --> 01:02:46,380
So you can collect the right kinds of data that you want.

1444
01:02:46,380 --> 01:02:49,000
And this is roughly where things are.

1445
01:02:49,000 --> 01:02:51,000
Of course, if you want to push the frontier out,

1446
01:02:51,000 --> 01:02:52,375
you don't get to necessarily play

1447
01:02:52,375 --> 01:02:55,020
these games, except in limited circumstances.

1448
01:02:55,020 --> 01:02:58,140
And so you're still very much reliant on human-driven data

1449
01:02:58,140 --> 01:03:00,940
collection.

1450
01:03:00,940 --> 01:03:01,740
OK.

1451
01:03:01,740 --> 01:03:02,240
Yeah.

1452
01:03:02,240 --> 01:03:05,820
[INAUDIBLE] example.

1453
01:03:05,820 --> 01:03:09,280
What is the [INAUDIBLE] model [INAUDIBLE]

1454
01:03:09,280 --> 01:03:11,660
because 7B is [INAUDIBLE] very small.

1455
01:03:11,660 --> 01:03:14,100
Well, 7B is small now.

1456
01:03:14,100 --> 01:03:16,580
I guess, at the time, I guess, 7B

1457
01:03:16,580 --> 01:03:19,200
was a very respectable open source model.

1458
01:03:19,200 --> 01:03:20,680
It's like Llama is a 7B.

1459
01:03:20,680 --> 01:03:22,420
Everyone loves Llama 7B.

1460
01:03:22,420 --> 01:03:24,380
We too would like to train a 7B.

1461
01:03:24,380 --> 01:03:26,600
And so in that sense, it was respectable.

1462
01:03:26,600 --> 01:03:31,020
I don't know if they went and tried it for their bigger runs,

1463
01:03:31,020 --> 01:03:32,500
but at least, at that scale, they

1464
01:03:32,500 --> 01:03:34,038
weren't seeing any differences.

1465
01:03:34,038 --> 01:03:36,580
I would be very surprised if they switched to the bigger one,

1466
01:03:36,580 --> 01:03:38,980
and suddenly there was a big difference.

1467
01:03:38,980 --> 01:03:39,860
Cool.

1468
01:03:39,860 --> 01:03:40,740
OK.

1469
01:03:40,740 --> 01:03:45,180
There are other places where you might do model generation that

1470
01:03:45,180 --> 01:03:46,780
is not purely distillation.

1471
01:03:46,780 --> 01:03:48,800
Anthropic had an early work on self-training.

1472
01:03:48,800 --> 01:03:51,340
This was Constitutional AI, where they kind of prompted

1473
01:03:51,340 --> 01:03:53,900
a model to generate safety data, and then

1474
01:03:53,900 --> 01:03:57,620
they trained the model itself on that prompted data.

1475
01:03:57,620 --> 01:04:01,000
And that allowed them to get a safer model out of the model.

1476
01:04:01,000 --> 01:04:05,140
And you can think of this as a very early self post-training

1477
01:04:05,140 --> 01:04:06,240
data generation loop.

1478
01:04:06,240 --> 01:04:08,540
And the other paper-- sorry, the other project

1479
01:04:08,540 --> 01:04:11,020
that I mentioned to you of a self-instruct, that's

1480
01:04:11,020 --> 01:04:13,500
a different kind of capability-centric version

1481
01:04:13,500 --> 01:04:14,520
of this kind of idea.

1482
01:04:14,520 --> 01:04:16,900
So you can use models to bootstrap

1483
01:04:16,900 --> 01:04:18,400
your own post-training data.

1484
01:04:18,400 --> 01:04:21,340
But if you want something like lawyers or scientists,

1485
01:04:21,340 --> 01:04:24,700
you can't get world knowledge without getting people

1486
01:04:24,700 --> 01:04:25,520
to annotate.

1487
01:04:25,520 --> 01:04:26,980
So you're kind of stuck, at least,

1488
01:04:26,980 --> 01:04:30,660
for that, with collecting human annotators.

1489
01:04:30,660 --> 01:04:33,820
The other thing I'll mention is that models are also

1490
01:04:33,820 --> 01:04:36,380
very susceptible to the same kinds of biases

1491
01:04:36,380 --> 01:04:40,540
as humans, sometimes in ways that are quite problematic as

1492
01:04:40,540 --> 01:04:42,380
well, even more problematic.

1493
01:04:42,380 --> 01:04:47,100
There's been a couple studies, after this kind of wave of model-

1494
01:04:47,100 --> 01:04:49,380
generated data, stuff started coming out,

1495
01:04:49,380 --> 01:04:52,500
showing that actually you could just push length

1496
01:04:52,500 --> 01:04:54,540
of your responses way out.

1497
01:04:54,540 --> 01:04:56,580
And you would continue to get improvements

1498
01:04:56,580 --> 01:05:02,180
in the win rates of model-judged performance.

1499
01:05:02,180 --> 01:05:04,940
And if you look, the one outlier here

1500
01:05:04,940 --> 01:05:07,897
is ChatGPT 3.5, basically saying,

1501
01:05:07,897 --> 01:05:09,480
well, they're not just length hacking,

1502
01:05:09,480 --> 01:05:11,780
they're actually a model that is actually better.

1503
01:05:11,780 --> 01:05:16,700
Similarly, others showed-- this was a very nice paper showing

1504
01:05:16,700 --> 01:05:19,040
that you can just RLHF on length alone,

1505
01:05:19,040 --> 01:05:23,460
and you could do quite well on many of these benchmarks.

1506
01:05:23,460 --> 01:05:27,460
So that's roughly the data story.

1507
01:05:27,460 --> 01:05:29,060
Who's collecting the data?

1508
01:05:29,060 --> 01:05:31,422
What kinds of things to be conscious of?

1509
01:05:31,422 --> 01:05:33,380
And then now in the remaining time that I have,

1510
01:05:33,380 --> 01:05:36,100
I'm going to talk about the algorithm.

1511
01:05:36,100 --> 01:05:38,420
I'm only going to briefly talk about PPO

1512
01:05:38,420 --> 01:05:40,100
because both in the interest of time

1513
01:05:40,100 --> 01:05:42,980
and because I'm just going to do a more extended treatment next

1514
01:05:42,980 --> 01:05:43,610
lecture.

1515
01:05:43,610 --> 01:05:45,360
And then I'll talk a little bit about DPO,

1516
01:05:45,360 --> 01:05:47,360
which is the cool fun bit.

1517
01:05:47,360 --> 01:05:47,860
OK.

1518
01:05:47,860 --> 01:05:51,940
So now, remember, we're in the RLHF regime.

1519
01:05:51,940 --> 01:05:53,400
The goal is the following.

1520
01:05:53,400 --> 01:05:57,260
I'm going to maximize, under my policy, the reward

1521
01:05:57,260 --> 01:05:59,120
that I get by sampling from that policy.

1522
01:05:59,120 --> 01:06:01,060
And this is like baby reinforcement learning.

1523
01:06:01,060 --> 01:06:03,900
Because people might say we're playing bandits.

1524
01:06:03,900 --> 01:06:05,960
This is not true multi-turn interesting RL.

1525
01:06:05,960 --> 01:06:07,480
And that's roughly right.

1526
01:06:07,480 --> 01:06:10,940
And so our algorithms will also be quite simple.

1527
01:06:10,940 --> 01:06:13,920
So the objective that I just showed you, maximize reward,

1528
01:06:13,920 --> 01:06:17,600
it appears almost exactly in the InstructGPT paper.

1529
01:06:17,600 --> 01:06:20,840
If you look at--open the paper up, and you go to equation 2,

1530
01:06:20,840 --> 01:06:23,100
you'll find exactly the kind of thing I wrote down,

1531
01:06:23,100 --> 01:06:25,720
where you sample from your RL policy,

1532
01:06:25,720 --> 01:06:29,760
I want to maximize my reward, subject to this second term,

1533
01:06:29,760 --> 01:06:31,820
which is really just a KL divergence.

1534
01:06:31,820 --> 01:06:33,540
All that it's saying is I want to stay

1535
01:06:33,540 --> 01:06:35,740
close to my pre-trained model because I

1536
01:06:35,740 --> 01:06:39,500
don't want to go too far and become degenerate.

1537
01:06:39,500 --> 01:06:42,520
Same thing is true from Stiennon, et al.

1538
01:06:42,520 --> 01:06:44,660
This is another paper.

1539
01:06:44,660 --> 01:06:48,487
You see, the reward in this case is a pairwise feedback model

1540
01:06:48,487 --> 01:06:49,320
that you've learned.

1541
01:06:49,320 --> 01:06:52,740
So you train a binary classifier on which of a pair of examples

1542
01:06:52,740 --> 01:06:53,520
is better.

1543
01:06:53,520 --> 01:06:55,740
And then you hill climb on that reward.

1544
01:06:55,740 --> 01:06:58,500
So very simple structure that we have.

1545
01:06:58,500 --> 01:07:00,800
Now, how do we actually do that hill climbing?

1546
01:07:00,800 --> 01:07:02,780
We use an algorithm called PPO.

1547
01:07:02,780 --> 01:07:06,100
If you've taken RL class, you, of course, know what PPO is.

1548
01:07:06,100 --> 01:07:08,700
If you haven't, I will give you a baby description

1549
01:07:08,700 --> 01:07:10,260
of what PPO is.

1550
01:07:10,260 --> 01:07:14,150
Also, you'll have to understand it for the assignment.

1551
01:07:14,150 --> 01:07:17,970
So the very basic starting point is the policy gradient identity.

1552
01:07:17,970 --> 01:07:20,710
What I want to do is I want to maximize my reward

1553
01:07:20,710 --> 01:07:22,210
sampling from my policy.

1554
01:07:22,210 --> 01:07:23,450
How do I optimize that?

1555
01:07:23,450 --> 01:07:26,010
Well, everything in machine learning is gradient descent.

1556
01:07:26,010 --> 01:07:27,210
So I take gradients.

1557
01:07:27,210 --> 01:07:29,590
So I want the gradient with respect to my parameters.

1558
01:07:29,590 --> 01:07:32,790
And you can push the gradient in and do the policy gradient

1559
01:07:32,790 --> 01:07:33,290
trick.

1560
01:07:33,290 --> 01:07:36,150
And you can show that that's equivalent to the equation on the right.

1561
01:07:36,150 --> 01:07:39,110
So I'm just going to take the gradient of my log probabilities

1562
01:07:39,110 --> 01:07:41,130
and weigh it by my rewards.

1563
01:07:41,130 --> 01:07:45,510
And this really just looks like SFT, but with weighted examples,

1564
01:07:45,510 --> 01:07:46,510
effectively.

1565
01:07:46,510 --> 01:07:49,610
Now, you might say, OK, policy gradients are great.

1566
01:07:49,610 --> 01:07:52,510
I love policy gradients, but the problem with policy gradients

1567
01:07:52,510 --> 01:07:54,790
is I need to sample every time I want

1568
01:07:54,790 --> 01:07:56,610
to take an optimization step.

1569
01:07:56,610 --> 01:07:57,910
And sampling is very expensive.

1570
01:07:57,910 --> 01:07:59,950
We've already talked about in the systems parts,

1571
01:07:59,950 --> 01:08:02,530
inference is often very complicated and hard,

1572
01:08:02,530 --> 01:08:05,990
whereas training is very arithmetically intense and good.

1573
01:08:05,990 --> 01:08:08,430
And so I want to roll out once, and I

1574
01:08:08,430 --> 01:08:10,650
want to reuse that rollout multiple times.

1575
01:08:10,650 --> 01:08:13,150
So this is something called off-policy.

1576
01:08:13,150 --> 01:08:16,109
To do off-policy, I want to take multiple steps

1577
01:08:16,109 --> 01:08:17,529
but not go too far.

1578
01:08:17,529 --> 01:08:20,830
Because if I go too far, my estimates of my local

1579
01:08:20,830 --> 01:08:22,370
rewards kind of blow up.

1580
01:08:22,370 --> 01:08:24,189
So this is something called TRPO.

1581
01:08:24,189 --> 01:08:27,090
The basic idea is I want to take policy gradients,

1582
01:08:27,090 --> 01:08:29,029
but I want to stay a little bit close to where

1583
01:08:29,029 --> 01:08:31,270
I am while you do something called an importance weighting

1584
01:08:31,270 --> 01:08:31,729
correction.

1585
01:08:31,729 --> 01:08:33,396
I'm not going to go into that in detail.

1586
01:08:33,396 --> 01:08:36,729
Finally, PPO says, well, TRPO is a good idea,

1587
01:08:36,729 --> 01:08:39,109
but this distance constraint is actually

1588
01:08:39,109 --> 01:08:40,502
kind of hard to deal with.

1589
01:08:40,502 --> 01:08:42,710
And so I'm going to come up with a heuristic clipping

1590
01:08:42,710 --> 01:08:45,390
thing that's just going to discourage the RL

1591
01:08:45,390 --> 01:08:48,630
algorithm from going to places where I'm too

1592
01:08:48,630 --> 01:08:50,410
far from the original policy.

1593
01:08:50,410 --> 01:08:53,510
I'll go through the details of this in the next lecture.

1594
01:08:53,510 --> 01:08:55,010
So you don't need to worry about it.

1595
01:08:55,010 --> 01:08:56,470
But the main progression of policy

1596
01:08:56,470 --> 01:08:59,410
gradient off-policy, you end up at PPO.

1597
01:08:59,410 --> 01:09:02,870
Hopefully, you roughly understand.

1598
01:09:02,870 --> 01:09:06,029
PPO，其实，这些公式看起来有点复杂。

1599
01:09:06,029 --> 01:09:08,069
所以很多人很多年来

1600
01:09:08,069 --> 01:09:09,649
都在问下面这个问题。

1601
01:09:09,649 --> 01:09:11,130
我们能去掉PPO吗？

1602
01:09:11,130 --> 01:09:14,790
这对很多人来说是一件大事。

1603
01:09:14,790 --> 01:09:16,710
很多有道理的人思考过

1604
01:09:16,710 --> 01:09:18,973
很多去掉PPO的好方法。

1605
01:09:18,973 --> 01:09:21,390
我会把它们告诉你，这样你就不一定

1606
01:09:21,390 --> 01:09:23,710
在自己的研究中重复它们。

1607
01:09:23,710 --> 01:09:26,790
例如，你可以说，我有一对例子，

1608
01:09:26,790 --> 01:09:30,270
我可以做SFT，但是用一种特殊的SFT。

1609
01:09:30,270 --> 01:09:33,810
对于好的那个例子，我会在前面加一个好token。

1610
01:09:33,810 --> 01:09:35,310
对于坏的那个例子，我

1611
01:09:35,310 --> 01:09:36,770
会在前面加一个坏token。

1612
01:09:36,770 --> 01:09:38,893
所以你会附加一些token。

1613
01:09:38,893 --> 01:09:40,310
然后当我生成时，我就

1614
01:09:40,310 --> 01:09:43,229
会在我的回复前面加上good，这样我的模型只会

1615
01:09:43,229 --> 01:09:44,170
生成好的内容。

1616
01:09:44,170 --> 01:09:47,310
所以这是把PPO简化为SFT，或者把RL简化为SFT。

1617
01:09:47,310 --> 01:09:49,229
这行不通。

1618
01:09:49,229 --> 01:09:52,229
人们尝试过只用好的内容来训练模型。

1619
01:09:52,229 --> 01:09:54,350
效果也不太好。

1620
01:09:54,350 --> 01:09:57,290
你可以训练奖励模型，获取LLM输出，

1621
01:09:57,290 --> 01:09:59,420
只选择奖励模型选中的内容

1622
01:09:59,420 --> 01:10:00,170
并在它们上训练。

1623
01:10:00,170 --> 01:10:03,590
这效果也不太好，虽然确实有点用。

1624
01:10:03,590 --> 01:10:07,150
所以很多人尝试过，很多不同的迭代。

1625
01:10:07,150 --> 01:10:10,990
最终，我们现在确实有了一个东西

1626
01:10:10,990 --> 01:10:14,790
比PPO简单得多，效果还不错。

1627
01:10:14,790 --> 01:10:17,910
而且在很多方面，看起来就像SFT。

1628
01:10:17,910 --> 01:10:19,870
这就是DPO。

1629
01:10:19,870 --> 01:10:22,168
所以这个算法非常非常简单。

1630
01:10:22,168 --> 01:10:23,710
我们要做的是，我们

1631
01:10:23,710 --> 01:10:25,797
要试着去掉PPO中复杂的部分。

1632
01:10:25,797 --> 01:10:27,130
我们要去掉什么？

1633
01:10:27,130 --> 01:10:28,950
我们要去掉奖励模型。

1634
01:10:28,950 --> 01:10:30,575
我们要去掉任何

1635
01:10:30,575 --> 01:10:32,025
与on-policy相关的东西。

1636
01:10:32,025 --> 01:10:33,650
通过去掉这两样东西，

1637
01:10:33,650 --> 01:10:39,190
我们要试着做一个更简单的RLHF算法。

1638
01:10:39,190 --> 01:10:41,890
And it relies on a very simple intuition.

1639
01:10:41,890 --> 01:10:44,030
Everything in deep learning is just

1640
01:10:44,030 --> 01:10:46,810
taking gradient steps in the direction of good things.

1641
01:10:46,810 --> 01:10:49,750
And so we're going to take steps in the direction of the log

1642
01:10:49,750 --> 01:10:51,010
loss of the good stuff.

1643
01:10:51,010 --> 01:10:53,070
And then we will take negative gradient steps

1644
01:10:53,070 --> 01:10:54,730
in the direction of the bad stuff.

1645
01:10:54,730 --> 01:10:58,710
In other words, we're going to reverse the log probability

1646
01:10:58,710 --> 01:11:00,590
of anything that is bad.

1647
01:11:00,590 --> 01:11:03,270
This is basically the most naive thing you can do.

1648
01:11:03,270 --> 01:11:05,030
Because you're doing SFT on one hand,

1649
01:11:05,030 --> 01:11:08,630
and you're doing kind of negative SFT on the bad thing.

1650
01:11:08,630 --> 01:11:11,210
It turns out that if you weight these two appropriately,

1651
01:11:11,210 --> 01:11:14,310
you will get an algorithm that is actually pretty good.

1652
01:11:14,310 --> 01:11:18,030
And I'll give you the derivation because it's all very simple.

1653
01:11:18,030 --> 01:11:21,570
So remember, our goal is to optimize this equation over here.

1654
01:11:21,570 --> 01:11:23,070
So this should look familiar to you.

1655
01:11:23,070 --> 01:11:26,990
The first term is my expected reward under my policy pi theta.

1656
01:11:26,990 --> 01:11:29,330
The second term is saying that's my KL distance.

1657
01:11:29,330 --> 01:11:33,450
I want my policy to remain close to my reference.

1658
01:11:33,450 --> 01:11:34,830
That's what I pretrain.

1659
01:11:34,830 --> 01:11:37,170
Now, I'm going to make an assumption.

1660
01:11:37,170 --> 01:11:40,350
This is the one strong assumption that I'm going to make.

1661
01:11:40,350 --> 01:11:42,790
And I'm going to assume that pi, my policy,

1662
01:11:42,790 --> 01:11:44,650
is actually not a neural network at all.

1663
01:11:44,650 --> 01:11:46,530
It is the set of all possible policies.

1664
01:11:46,530 --> 01:11:48,050
It is a nonparametric thing.

1665
01:11:48,050 --> 01:11:49,610
It can approximate anything.

1666
01:11:49,610 --> 01:11:52,990
So if pi can be anything, I can actually

1667
01:11:52,990 --> 01:11:55,450
solve that top problem closed form.

1668
01:11:55,450 --> 01:11:58,110
I actually know what the minimizer is.

1669
01:11:58,110 --> 01:12:00,750
And the minimizer is the following.

1670
01:12:00,750 --> 01:12:04,510
The minimizer is actually to take my original reference

1671
01:12:04,510 --> 01:12:08,290
policy, and to exponentially tilt it by my reward.

1672
01:12:08,290 --> 01:12:12,670
In other words, every response gets upweighted or downweighted

1673
01:12:12,670 --> 01:12:16,390
with a weight, e to the 1 over beta r

1674
01:12:16,390 --> 01:12:17,770
based on how good the reward is.

1675
01:12:17,770 --> 01:12:18,610
This makes a lot of sense.

1676
01:12:18,610 --> 01:12:20,010
If the reward is really bad, I'm going

1677
01:12:20,010 --> 01:12:21,210
to exponentially downweight.

1678
01:12:21,210 --> 01:12:22,190
If the reward is really good, I'm

1679
01:12:22,190 --> 01:12:23,648
going to exponentially upweight it.

1680
01:12:23,648 --> 01:12:27,230
So this is the closed form solution if pi can be anything.

1681
01:12:27,230 --> 01:12:29,230
Now, we can then--

1682
01:12:29,230 --> 01:12:32,650
now that this pi of r, this perfect solution,

1683
01:12:32,650 --> 01:12:34,850
I can solve for the implied reward.

1684
01:12:34,850 --> 01:12:36,790
In other words, I can solve for what is

1685
01:12:36,790 --> 01:12:40,090
the r that would induce this.

1686
01:12:40,090 --> 01:12:41,410
And so I can solve for that.

1687
01:12:41,410 --> 01:12:43,010
I can put the pi on the left side,

1688
01:12:43,010 --> 01:12:48,430
and I can solve for the reward that we would have.

1689
01:12:48,430 --> 01:12:52,550
And then we can take that implied reward that I wrote down.

1690
01:12:52,550 --> 01:12:54,670
And then I can solve for what would happen

1691
01:12:54,670 --> 01:12:56,610
in the original RLHF objective.

1692
01:12:56,610 --> 01:13:00,310
I plug in my reward into that objective here, into my loss.

1693
01:13:00,310 --> 01:13:04,350
And this gives the DPO objective, which is very intuitive.

1694
01:13:04,350 --> 01:13:09,872
It basically says take gradient steps in the positive direction,

1695
01:13:09,872 --> 01:13:12,330
and then take negative gradient steps in the bad direction.

1696
01:13:12,330 --> 01:13:14,205
You can already see what's going to happen.

1697
01:13:14,205 --> 01:13:16,830
This first term over here, this is the good stuff.

1698
01:13:16,830 --> 01:13:19,050
This is the bad stuff on the right term here.

1699
01:13:19,050 --> 01:13:21,710
And they're going to be balancing each other.

1700
01:13:21,710 --> 01:13:22,707
OK.

1701
01:13:22,707 --> 01:13:24,290
And so if I take the gradient of this,

1702
01:13:24,290 --> 01:13:26,450
I think this is the most intuitive form of DPO,

1703
01:13:26,450 --> 01:13:29,070
at least to me, you have two things.

1704
01:13:29,070 --> 01:13:31,950
For every pair that you've had annotated,

1705
01:13:31,950 --> 01:13:34,430
I'm going to increase the likelihood of the example,

1706
01:13:34,430 --> 01:13:36,790
that one, and I'm going to decrease the likelihood

1707
01:13:36,790 --> 01:13:39,470
of the example that lost.

1708
01:13:39,470 --> 01:13:44,630
And I'm going to scale the step size in some sense

1709
01:13:44,630 --> 01:13:48,570
by how much my implied reward model is wrong.

1710
01:13:48,570 --> 01:13:51,750
Did my model already assign a very high reward

1711
01:13:51,750 --> 01:13:52,730
to the winning one?

1712
01:13:52,730 --> 01:13:55,010
If it did so, I will take a small step.

1713
01:13:55,010 --> 01:13:57,750
But if I was very wrong, and I said, oh, these two are almost

1714
01:13:57,750 --> 01:13:59,770
equal, I had same probability.

1715
01:13:59,770 --> 01:14:01,830
But in that case, I will actually

1716
01:14:01,830 --> 01:14:03,190
take a much bigger step size.

1717
01:14:03,190 --> 01:14:05,210
So based on the probability differences,

1718
01:14:05,210 --> 01:14:08,950
I will take bigger or smaller step sizes on this differential

1719
01:14:08,950 --> 01:14:10,410
log prob objective.

1720
01:14:10,410 --> 01:14:13,990
So hopefully, the action of this gradient objective

1721
01:14:13,990 --> 01:14:16,890
is quite clear to people.

1722
01:14:16,890 --> 01:14:17,390
OK.

1723
01:14:17,390 --> 01:14:20,620
So this is actually much, much simpler than PPO

1724
01:14:20,620 --> 01:14:22,370
because all I'm doing is taking gradients.

1725
01:14:22,370 --> 01:14:26,670
And we all know how to do that with our standard processes.

1726
01:14:26,670 --> 01:14:29,270
And I think the nice thing is that it does work reasonably

1727
01:14:29,270 --> 01:14:29,770
well.

1728
01:14:29,770 --> 01:14:34,110
I think for a while, people were very obsessed with, is DPO

1729
01:14:34,110 --> 01:14:35,930
better than PPO or vice versa?

1730
01:14:35,930 --> 01:14:38,618
I think the answer now is maybe it doesn't matter very much,

1731
01:14:38,618 --> 01:14:40,410
unless you're like at the frontier training

1732
01:14:40,410 --> 01:14:41,670
the very best model.

1733
01:14:41,670 --> 01:14:43,350
DPO is reasonably good.

1734
01:14:43,350 --> 01:14:44,810
And it's good enough for LLaMA.

1735
01:14:44,810 --> 01:14:46,070
It's good enough for me.

1736
01:14:46,070 --> 01:14:48,170
If you go and open up the LLaMA tech report,

1737
01:14:48,170 --> 01:14:52,110
you'll find that they basically had this nice outer loop

1738
01:14:52,110 --> 01:14:55,370
training, where they had their model, they SFT'd it,

1739
01:14:55,370 --> 01:14:56,550
they did DPO.

1740
01:14:56,550 --> 01:14:58,670
And then they used that DPO model

1741
01:14:58,670 --> 01:15:01,870
to then generate candidates that they rejection sampled and they

1742
01:15:01,870 --> 01:15:02,810
repeated this process.

1743
01:15:02,810 --> 01:15:04,490
So there's the outer loop on top of it.

1744
01:15:04,490 --> 01:15:09,750
But basically, the core RLHF primitive for Llama was DPO.

1745
01:15:09,750 --> 01:15:11,550
In the years since, there's been a lot

1746
01:15:11,550 --> 01:15:14,810
of different variants of DPO.

1747
01:15:14,810 --> 01:15:18,490
There was SimPO, which basically changes the weighting.

1748
01:15:18,490 --> 01:15:23,630
So instead of using pi ref, you have this y of l normalizer.

1749
01:15:23,630 --> 01:15:27,750
There was length normalized DPO, which normalizes by the length

1750
01:15:27,750 --> 01:15:30,150
in order to avoid certain length hacking

1751
01:15:30,150 --> 01:15:32,590
issues that seem to arise.

1752
01:15:32,590 --> 01:15:35,510
But really, none of these variants

1753
01:15:35,510 --> 01:15:38,550
maybe seem to matter very much.

1754
01:15:38,550 --> 01:15:40,230
One of the things that I'll mention,

1755
01:15:40,230 --> 01:15:42,150
that I thought was very interesting,

1756
01:15:42,150 --> 01:15:45,310
was these results are very, very contingent on the specifics

1757
01:15:45,310 --> 01:15:46,530
of the experiment setup.

1758
01:15:46,530 --> 01:15:50,810
So even at AI2, there was a paper that was like, well,

1759
01:15:50,810 --> 01:15:53,670
it turns out that if you go from DPO to PPO,

1760
01:15:53,670 --> 01:15:57,510
you'll get a better result. But then they also

1761
01:15:57,510 --> 01:16:01,210
had a paper for Tulu2, where they said, actually,

1762
01:16:01,210 --> 01:16:05,270
if you do DPO right, you can do better than PPO.

1763
01:16:05,270 --> 01:16:08,555
And so depending on how you execute this,

1764
01:16:08,555 --> 01:16:09,930
one can be better than the other.

1765
01:16:09,930 --> 01:16:12,070
I'm sure you have experiences reading deep learning

1766
01:16:12,070 --> 01:16:15,630
papers and so on, where the results are very fragile.

1767
01:16:15,630 --> 01:16:20,347
So maybe the takeaway from all of this that these DPO variants are actually

1768
01:16:20,347 --> 01:16:22,430
close enough to the right thing to give you pretty

1769
01:16:22,430 --> 01:16:24,110
good performance in many cases.

1770
01:16:24,110 --> 01:16:26,910
This core idea of take gradient step in the right direction

1771
01:16:26,910 --> 01:16:30,030
and take negative gradient step from the bad stuff

1772
01:16:30,030 --> 01:16:33,710
works reasonably well as long as you set the step sizes right.

1773
01:16:33,710 --> 01:16:35,030
OK.

1774
01:16:35,030 --> 01:16:37,430
I will end with the last few slides on things

1775
01:16:37,430 --> 01:16:39,630
to watch out for in RLHF.

1776
01:16:39,630 --> 01:16:43,170
So one of the things to watch out for is over optimization.

1777
01:16:43,170 --> 01:16:45,790
And this is maybe one of the biggest things.

1778
01:16:45,790 --> 01:16:48,270
To give you also historical context,

1779
01:16:48,270 --> 01:16:51,830
when InstructGPT came out, and people were very excited

1780
01:16:51,830 --> 01:16:53,990
about this, I think there was a very real question

1781
01:16:53,990 --> 01:16:58,610
can we RLHF our way to superintelligent systems or whatever?

1782
01:16:58,610 --> 01:16:59,152
Maybe we can.

1783
01:16:59,152 --> 01:17:01,430
Just collect enough thumbs up, thumbs down.

1784
01:17:01,430 --> 01:17:04,310
Turns out that is actually quite challenging.

1785
01:17:04,310 --> 01:17:08,430
Because you find that if you try to really push the RLHF

1786
01:17:08,430 --> 01:17:11,070
process forward, you'll start overfitting

1787
01:17:11,070 --> 01:17:12,710
to your learned reward model.

1788
01:17:12,710 --> 01:17:14,910
So the KL regularizer that I talked about

1789
01:17:14,910 --> 01:17:18,030
is really critical in a lot of cases in order

1790
01:17:18,030 --> 01:17:20,870
to prevent your optimization process

1791
01:17:20,870 --> 01:17:23,710
from overfitting your reward model,

1792
01:17:23,710 --> 01:17:27,470
at least if your optimization process is very good.

1793
01:17:27,470 --> 01:17:31,870
The other thing that I'll talk about is model collapse.

1794
01:17:31,870 --> 01:17:35,670
So people have seen lots of times, where RL models have

1795
01:17:35,670 --> 01:17:36,810
much less diversity.

1796
01:17:36,810 --> 01:17:40,270
They're concentrated on a few different possible outputs.

1797
01:17:40,270 --> 01:17:42,870
And this connects back to what I said before right RLHF

1798
01:17:42,870 --> 01:17:45,870
models are really no longer modeling

1799
01:17:45,870 --> 01:17:49,130
a distribution which comes with its own inherent diversity.

1800
01:17:49,130 --> 01:17:50,670
It's a policy that can collapse as

1801
01:17:50,670 --> 01:17:52,230
long as it gets a good reward.

1802
01:17:52,230 --> 01:17:56,150
So this is another thing that RLHF models have really

1803
01:17:56,150 --> 01:18:00,670
struggled with in recent years.

1804
01:18:00,670 --> 01:18:04,270
So actually, I'm going to skip this because we're at time.

1805
01:18:04,270 --> 01:18:06,830
And I think this entropy and mode collapse thing actually

1806
01:18:06,830 --> 01:18:10,670
ends up being quite important even today.

1807
01:18:10,670 --> 01:18:13,670
And the GPT-4 era actually was one of the few plots

1808
01:18:13,670 --> 01:18:15,830
that OpenAI put out, where they said, actually, we

1809
01:18:15,830 --> 01:18:17,480
have a few open problems left.

1810
01:18:17,480 --> 01:18:19,230
And one of the open problems they had was,

1811
01:18:19,230 --> 01:18:20,772
actually, our models are uncalibrated

1812
01:18:20,772 --> 01:18:24,270
after we do RLHF, which you can see in this plot over here.

1813
01:18:24,270 --> 01:18:27,310
I don't think anyone has really solved that yet.

1814
01:18:27,310 --> 01:18:30,970
The Anthropic folks also had an argument saying, well,

1815
01:18:30,970 --> 01:18:32,330
it's naturally uncalibrated.

1816
01:18:32,330 --> 01:18:34,850
You could recalibrate sometimes, but not always.

1817
01:18:34,850 --> 01:18:37,530
This becomes very important in the next lecture

1818
01:18:37,530 --> 01:18:39,910
when we talk about things like RLVR,

1819
01:18:39,910 --> 01:18:42,430
where the entropy and exploration is actually

1820
01:18:42,430 --> 01:18:44,510
quite critical for the model to explore

1821
01:18:44,510 --> 01:18:47,070
all the possible solutions, and to make progress on some

1822
01:18:47,070 --> 01:18:49,990
of these very hard problems.

1823
01:18:49,990 --> 01:18:51,750
Great.

1824
01:18:51,750 --> 01:18:56,230
So to put it all together, RLHF data collection

1825
01:18:56,230 --> 01:18:57,370
is also very hard.

1826
01:18:57,370 --> 01:18:59,270
I think part of the thing about post-training

1827
01:18:59,270 --> 01:19:02,390
is it's very complicated, messy process because a lot of it

1828
01:19:02,390 --> 01:19:03,830
is getting good data.

1829
01:19:03,830 --> 01:19:06,870
And getting good data is always very difficult.

1830
01:19:06,870 --> 01:19:09,290
RLHF algorithms are quite complex.

1831
01:19:09,290 --> 01:19:12,230
PPO is especially difficult. I'll talk a little bit about it

1832
01:19:12,230 --> 01:19:13,270
next lecture.

1833
01:19:13,270 --> 01:19:15,530
Thankfully, we do also have a simpler variant.

1834
01:19:15,530 --> 01:19:16,492
They're called GRPO.

1835
01:19:16,492 --> 01:19:17,450
That works pretty well.

1836
01:19:17,450 --> 01:19:19,283
That's what you'll do in your assignments.

1837
01:19:19,283 --> 01:19:20,950
But this is one place where there's been

1838
01:19:20,950 --> 01:19:22,870
a lot of recent developments.

1839
01:19:22,870 --> 01:19:25,130
Finally, I think, in terms of RLHF,

1840
01:19:25,130 --> 01:19:28,767
one of the big, big problems is over-optimization.

1841
01:19:28,767 --> 01:19:30,350
And the transition to the next lecture

1842
01:19:30,350 --> 01:19:34,390
is going to be: is there rewards where we won't over-optimize

1843
01:19:34,390 --> 01:19:37,390
or we can just dumping compute and the model performance just

1844
01:19:37,390 --> 01:19:38,850
keeps monotonically getting better.

1845
01:19:38,850 --> 01:19:41,230
And that's one of the reasons why what people call

1846
01:19:41,230 --> 01:19:45,110
RLVR is just so been so impactful.

1847
01:19:45,110 --> 01:19:45,630
Thanks.

